const e=[{id:1,code:"FIRE-101",icon:"Flame",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire science and combustion dynamics",subtitle:"Fire Science & Combustion Dynamics",summary:"A chemical and physical analysis of fire: the fire tetrahedron, heat transfer mechanisms, the stages of fire development and the flashover and backdraft phenomena.",blocks:[{body:`Introduction: Fire is not merely a visible phenomenon; in engineering and chemistry it is defined as a process of rapid oxidation in which energy is released in the form of heat and light. Since ancient times humanity has used fire to advance civilization, but when fire is not properly controlled it becomes a destructive force. For safety engineers and civil defense specialists, understanding combustion dynamics is not only necessary; it is the fundamental basis for protecting life and property.

The scientific nature of fire: Fire is a complex exothermic (heat-releasing) chemical reaction. The process requires the correct balance between three or four principal elements. The difference between a controlled fire and a fire disaster depends on our understanding of how heat spreads and of the physical changes that materials undergo.

Aim of this module: We will move beyond a basic familiarity with fire toward a more detailed study of (1) the chemistry of combustion: how the molecules of a material break down and become combustible gases; (2) the physics of energy transfer: how heat moves by conduction, convection and radiation; (3) fire behavior in enclosed spaces: an analysis of lethal phenomena such as flashover and backdraft. This knowledge is fundamental for every university student or engineer who intends to design protection systems and firefighting strategies on a scientific basis.`},{heading:"1. Fire tetrahedron theory: a dynamic analysis",body:'At the academic and engineering level, we move beyond the concept of the "fire triangle" and rely on the "fire tetrahedron." The key difference is that the tetrahedron shows how flaming combustion functions as a self-sustaining chemical process. If any one of these four elements is removed, or their balance is disrupted, the fire will go out.',points:["Fuel (reducing agent): fuel can exist in any of the three physical states (solid, liquid, gas). An important scientific note: solids and liquids do not themselves burn with a flame; a solid must first undergo thermal decomposition (pyrolysis) and a liquid must first vaporize. The surface-to-mass ratio is very important: the finer the material, the faster it enters the combustion process.","Oxidizing agent: in most situations, atmospheric oxygen (approximately 21%) is the main source. In general, if the oxygen concentration falls below approximately 16% (the exact figure depends on the fuel and the temperature), flaming combustion weakens or stops and becomes smoldering combustion. An advanced concept: some materials (such as ammonium nitrate) are themselves oxidizers and supply oxygen to the fire, which makes them very difficult to extinguish.","Heat (activation energy): the energy that initiates and sustains combustion. Thermal feedback is the physical phenomenon in which the fire radiates heat back to the fuel that has not yet burned. The autoignition temperature is the lowest temperature at which a material ignites by itself, without a spark or flame.","Uninhibited chemical chain reaction: this element turns a momentary flame into sustained combustion. Free radicals such as H, O and OH react very rapidly with new molecules and release energy. Some extinguishing agents (such as Halon and dry chemical powder) remove these radicals and thereby break the chain reaction.","Scientific notes: Vapor pressure: the higher it is, the more readily a material gives off vapor and the more easily it ignites. Flammability limits: fire occurs only within a suitable range of fuel-to-air ratio; if the gas concentration is too high (rich) or too low (lean), ignition will not occur."]},{heading:"2. Heat transfer mechanisms",body:"Fire does not stay in one place; it spreads by three different mechanisms and transfers energy to other materials. Understanding these mechanisms is essential for engineers in order to know how a fire grows and how it can be prevented:",points:["Conduction: the transfer of heat through direct contact within solid materials. For example, if one side of an iron door or a metal wall is heated by fire, the heat travels through the material to the other side and can bring materials on that side to their ignition temperature. Metals (such as iron, copper and aluminum) conduct heat very well (high thermal conductivity), whereas wood, plastic and concrete conduct it less well. This is one of the reasons why fire-resistant walls are built from materials of low conductivity.","Convection: the transfer of heat through the movement of liquids or gases. Hot air and combustion gases are lighter than cool air and therefore rise (the principle of buoyancy). This is one of the most dangerous mechanisms in enclosed buildings, because hot air, smoke and toxic gases accumulate beneath the ceiling and can reach about 600–800 °C, depending on the fuel. When this hot layer reaches materials at a high level, it brings them to their ignition point and causes the fire to spread to other rooms and floors.","Radiation: the transfer of heat energy in the form of electromagnetic waves, particularly infrared radiation. Radiation requires neither direct contact nor air; energy is transferred even through a vacuum. A large fire can therefore bring a nearby building to its ignition temperature without any flame reaching it. For this reason, firefighters wear specialized protective clothing and maintain a safe distance from the fire."]},{heading:"3. Stages of fire development",body:"Every fire, from a small fire on a stove to a fire in a large building, generally passes through four standard stages. Recognizing these stages is essential for firefighters in order to know when to intervene and with which strategy:",points:["Incipient stage: the earliest stage of the fire. The temperature is still low, smoke is present but limited and the fire is still small and confined to one place. At this stage a trained person can control the fire with a portable extinguisher, after the alarm has been raised and 115 called. The oxygen concentration is still approximately 21%. This is the best time to extinguish the fire, because it is still small.","Growth stage: the fire grows rapidly; heat increases, smoke becomes heavy and flames reach nearby materials. The temperature reaches several hundred degrees Celsius and hot gases accumulate beneath the ceiling. At this stage there is a risk of rollover: the ignition of unburned gases in the hot layer beneath the ceiling, which spread as tongues of flame across the ceiling. Without intervention, the fire moves rapidly to the next stage.","Fully developed stage: the hottest and most dangerous stage. After flashover, all the combustible materials in the room have ignited and burn simultaneously. The temperature can exceed 1,000 °C, depending on the fuel. Flames emerge from doors and windows and black smoke fills the entire space. Entering the room at this stage is extremely dangerous and is carried out only with full protection and a tactical plan. The fire consumes most of the available oxygen, so combustion becomes ventilation-limited.","Decay stage: once most of the fuel has burned or the oxygen has been depleted, the fire gradually weakens and the temperature falls, but a major danger still remains: backdraft. If a room is filled with unburned combustible gases and someone suddenly opens the door so that oxygen enters, a massive explosion can occur that may kill everyone nearby."]},{heading:"4. Dangerous fire phenomena",body:"In fire science there are two lethal phenomena that every engineer and firefighter must fully understand:",points:["Flashover: the sudden, simultaneous ignition of all the combustible materials in a room. It generally occurs when the temperature of the gas layer beneath the ceiling reaches approximately 500–600 °C and the radiant heat from the ceiling brings every material in the room to its ignition point. This transition occurs within a very short time (a few seconds) and turns the room into a furnace. Warning signs of flashover: thick black smoke beneath the ceiling, tongues of flame beneath the ceiling (rollover), smoke being given off by furnishings and a sudden intense rise in heat. When firefighters see these signs, they must leave the room immediately or cool the gas layer with water.","Backdraft: a fire explosion that occurs when oxygen suddenly enters a hot, oxygen-depleted room that still contains large quantities of combustible gases and heat. Such a room is like a bomb waiting for oxygen. When the door is opened, fresh air rushes in, the combustible gases ignite all at once and an explosion occurs that propels a large fireball outward. Warning signs of backdraft: yellowish-brown smoke, windows blackened by smoke, a hot door, smoke pulsing in and out (pulsating smoke) and a whistling sound from the room. In these situations the door must not simply be opened; first, ventilation must be carried out from the highest point (vertical ventilation), or the door must be opened in a controlled manner from a protected position."]},{heading:"5. Thermal decomposition (pyrolysis)",body:"Pyrolysis is the chemical process in which solid materials break down under heat and become combustible gases. In scientific terms, a solid material does not itself burn with a flame; it is the gases produced by pyrolysis that burn. For example, wood itself does not ignite, but when it reaches high temperatures (approximately 200–300 °C, depending on the type of wood) its molecules break down and produce combustible volatile gases, and these vapors ignite and burn. The residue of this process is char, which consists mostly of carbon.",points:["During pyrolysis, plastic materials produce highly toxic gases such as hydrogen cyanide (HCN) and carbon monoxide (CO).","Materials such as upholstery foam and polystyrene undergo pyrolysis very rapidly and produce hazardous gases.","Knowing the pyrolysis temperature of materials is important for the design of building materials and furniture that are difficult to ignite."]},{heading:"6. Products of combustion and toxicity",body:"When materials burn, various gases are produced, some of which are lethal:",points:["Carbon monoxide (CO): an odorless, colorless but highly toxic gas. It binds to the hemoglobin in the blood and prevents oxygen from being transported to the tissues and the brain. Most fire deaths are caused by inhaling smoke and toxic gases (such as CO and HCN), not by burns to the body.","Carbon dioxide (CO₂): not a toxic gas at low concentrations, but at high concentrations it becomes toxic in its own right and also causes asphyxiation because it displaces oxygen.","Hydrogen cyanide (HCN): an extremely lethal gas produced by the combustion of plastics, foam and certain fabrics. At high concentrations it can kill within a very short time.","Hydrogen chloride (HCl): a corrosive gas produced by burning PVC (the plastic used in pipes and cable insulation); it damages the lungs and eyes.","Smoke: smoke is not merely a color; it is full of fine particulates, gases and chemical substances. Smoke can cause asphyxiation, temporary blindness and lung damage."]},{heading:"7. Fire classification: the NFPA system and the European EN 2 system",body:"To select the correct extinguisher, you must know the classification of fires. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. Each class requires a specific method of extinguishment:",points:["Class A (both systems) — ordinary solid combustibles: wood, paper, fabric, ordinary plastics. Extinguishment: cooling with water. Water, foam and ABC powder extinguishers are suitable.","Class B — flammable liquids: gasoline, oil, paint, solvents (under NFPA, flammable gases are also included in Class B; under EN 2, gases are Class C). Extinguishment (liquids): smothering with foam, CO₂ or ABC powder. A direct stream of water is unsuitable because it spreads the burning liquid. For alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam; ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.","Class C (NFPA) — fires involving energized electrical equipment: panels, cables, appliances. Extinguishment: non-conductive agents such as CO₂ or dry chemical powder. If it is safe to do so, de-energize the equipment first. In the EN 2 system, electrical fires are not a separate class and Class C refers to flammable gases.","Class D (both systems) — combustible metals: magnesium, titanium, sodium, potassium, lithium (metal). Extinguishment: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal (Class D powders such as graphite-based or sodium chloride-based powder); the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam: water is extremely dangerous because it can cause a violent reaction and an explosion. Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry. Note: lithium-ion batteries contain no metallic lithium; for fires involving them, water and continuous cooling are recommended.","Class K (NFPA) / Class F (EN 2) — cooking oils and fats: in restaurant and home kitchens. Extinguishment: a wet chemical extinguisher, which produces saponification: the oil turns into a soap-like layer that excludes oxygen and extinguishes the fire. Never pour water on burning oil."]},{heading:"8. An important note for students",body:"Understanding the fire tetrahedron is the key to selecting the correct extinguishing agent. If any one of the four elements of the tetrahedron is removed or interrupted, the fire goes out:",points:["Remove the fuel: move combustible materials away from the fire area, or shut off the gas supply.","Remove the heat: cool with water or with cooling agents.","Remove the oxygen: smother and cover the fire (for example with foam or a fire blanket).","Break the chain reaction: use dry chemical powder or special agents."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_fire_dynamics.mp4",title:"Fire dynamics",description:"A complete explanation of the stages of fire development and how fire spreads"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_tetrahedron.png",title:"The fire tetrahedron",description:"A complete diagram of the fire tetrahedron and its four principal elements"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_flashover.mp4",title:"The flashover phenomenon",description:"A laboratory test demonstrating flashover"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_heat_transfer.png",title:"Heat transfer mechanisms",description:"Conduction, convection and radiation, illustrated"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_backdraft.mp4",title:"The danger of backdraft",description:"How to recognize the warning signs"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_01_fire_classes.png",title:"Fire classification",description:"A table of fire classes according to NFPA and EN 2, with the class symbols"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_01_extinguisher_types.mp4",title:"Types of extinguishers",description:"How to select and use the correct extinguisher"}],quiz:[{question:"Which element is added to the fire triangle to form the fire tetrahedron?",options:[{text:"Heat",correct:!1},{text:"The chemical chain reaction",correct:!0},{text:"Oxygen",correct:!1},{text:"Fuel",correct:!1}],explanation:"The chemical chain reaction is the fourth element that completes the fire tetrahedron. This process sustains the fire and allows it to propagate by itself."},{question:"Flashover marks the change from which stage to which?",options:[{text:"From incipient to growth",correct:!1},{text:"From growth to fully developed",correct:!0},{text:"From fully developed to decay",correct:!1},{text:"From decay to incipient",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flashover generally occurs at the end of the growth stage, when the temperature rises and all materials approach their ignition point, and carries the fire into the fully developed stage."},{question:"Which gas kills the most people in fires?",options:[{text:"Oxygen",correct:!1},{text:"Carbon monoxide (CO)",correct:!0},{text:"Nitrogen",correct:!1},{text:"Hydrogen",correct:!1}],explanation:"Carbon monoxide (CO) is the principal lethal gas in fires, because it is odorless and colorless and rapidly stops the transport of oxygen in the blood."},{question:"What is pyrolysis?",options:[{text:"Extinguishing a fire",correct:!1},{text:"The thermal decomposition of a material into combustible vapors",correct:!0},{text:"Explosion",correct:!1},{text:"Cooling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Pyrolysis is a chemical process in which materials break down under heat and become combustible gases; these gases then ignite and burn."},{question:"Which extinguisher is best for a Class B fire (flammable liquids)?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"Foam or CO₂",correct:!0},{text:"Soil",correct:!1},{text:"Air",correct:!1}],explanation:"Flammable liquid fires (Class B) require smothering. Foam or CO₂ is suitable because it cuts off the oxygen. Water is unsuitable because it spreads the burning liquid."},{question:"Which of the following is a warning sign of backdraft?",options:[{text:"White smoke",correct:!1},{text:"Yellowish-brown smoke and a hot door",correct:!0},{text:"The room cooling down",correct:!1},{text:"Light",correct:!1}],explanation:"Backdraft has specific warning signs: yellowish-brown smoke, a hot door and smoke pulsing in and out. These indicate that combustible gases are present in the room and are waiting for oxygen."}]},{id:2,code:"CHEM-202",icon:"FlaskConical",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Hazardous materials (HAZMAT) and chemical safety",subtitle:"Hazardous Materials Management & Chemical Safety",summary:"Identifying hazardous materials, the Globally Harmonized System (GHS), the NFPA 704 diamond, responding to chemical spills in laboratories and factories, and personal protection.",blocks:[{body:`In today's world we use thousands of chemical substances every day: in laboratories, factories, hospitals and even at home. Yet many of these substances are dangerous if they are used or stored incorrectly. Chemicals can poison, burn, explode or eat away and destroy other materials (corrosion). For this reason, everyone who works with chemicals, whether a laboratory student, a factory worker or an engineer, must be fully aware of the hazards and of the means of self-protection.

In this module we examine the subject of chemical safety. We will learn how to identify substances using standard systems such as GHS and NFPA 704, how to read a Safety Data Sheet (SDS) and what steps to take in the event of a chemical spill or incident.`},{heading:"1. The Globally Harmonized System (GHS) for hazard identification",body:"GHS is an international system developed under the auspices of the United Nations (UN) for classifying and labeling hazardous substances in a uniform way throughout the world. Its nine pictograms are as follows:",points:["Flame: flammable substances such as acetone, gasoline and alcohol.","Skull and Crossbones: substances with high acute toxicity.","Corrosion: acids and bases that burn the skin and corrode metals.","Exploding Bomb: explosive or unstable substances.","Exclamation Mark: less severe health hazards (such as skin irritation).","Environment: harm to the aquatic environment and to living organisms.","Health Hazard: long-term hazards (such as cancer and lung damage).","Gas Under Pressure: gases stored under pressure (cylinders).","Oxidizer: oxidizing substances that intensify fire."]},{heading:"2. The NFPA 704 diamond (fire diamond)",body:"A visual system for rapid hazard identification, displayed on doors and storage areas. Each of the blue, red and yellow sections carries a number from 0 to 4 (4 = the highest hazard):",points:["Blue — Health Hazard (0–4): the health hazard.","Red — Flammability (0–4): the fire hazard.","Yellow — Instability (0–4): instability and reactivity.","White — Special Hazards: W (reacts dangerously with water), OX (oxidizer), SA (simple asphyxiant gas)."]},{heading:"3. The Safety Data Sheet (SDS): 16 sections",body:"Every hazardous chemical must have its own SDS. Its most important sections for emergency response are:",points:["Section 1: Identification.","Section 2: Hazard Identification.","Section 3: Composition.","Section 4: First Aid.","Section 5: Fire-fighting Measures.","Section 6: Accidental Release Measures.","Section 7: Handling and Storage.","Section 8: Exposure Controls and Personal Protection (PPE)."]},{heading:"4. Steps for a small chemical spill",body:"Structured steps for controlling a small spill, for trained laboratory users only. Identify the substance and put on PPE before you approach the spill. Important note: if the spill is large, the substance is unknown or highly toxic, or there is an odor or fire, do not attempt to control it yourself: evacuate the area and call 115.",points:["1. Stop and warn the people nearby; keep them away.","2. Identify the substance from the label or the SDS, from a safe distance.","3. Evaluate: clean it up yourself only if the spill is small, the substance is known and low-hazard, and you are trained for it. Otherwise leave, close the door behind you and call 115.","4. Put on the PPE the SDS lists and get the spill kit.","5. Contain the spill from the edges inward and clean it up with the kit.","6. Inform the person in charge and record the incident."]},{heading:"5. Personal protective equipment (PPE)",body:"Types of PPE for working with chemicals; the correct selection is made according to Section 8 of the SDS:",points:["Gloves: nitrile or neoprene for most chemicals; the correct type is chosen according to the SDS and the chemical resistance chart.","Safety glasses with side shields, or sealed goggles where there is a splash hazard.","Respiratory protection: an N95 mask filters only fine particles and provides no protection against chemical vapors and gases. For vapors and gases, a respirator fitted with cartridges suitable for that substance, as specified in the SDS, is required; for unknown substances, high concentrations or oxygen deficiency, only a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA).","Laboratory coat: flame-resistant in areas where flammable substances are present.","Closed-toe shoes that protect the whole foot."]},{heading:"6. Chemical storage",body:"Rules for safe storage:",points:["Segregation: keep acids away from bases, and oxidizers away from flammable substances.","Temperature: if flammable substances need refrigeration, store them only in special explosion-proof refrigerators, never in an ordinary refrigerator.","Locking: keep toxic substances in locked cabinets.","Labeling: label every bottle and container with its name and GHS pictograms.","Lower shelves: keep heavy substances and glass containers on low shelves."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_02_ghs_symbols.png",title:"GHS pictograms",description:"The complete list of international pictograms"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_02_spill_cleanup.mp4",title:"Spill cleanup",description:"Using a spill kit"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_02_nfpa704.png",title:"NFPA 704",description:"An explanation of the colors and numbers"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_02_ppe_demo.mp4",title:"Donning PPE",description:"The correct way to put on and take off PPE"}],quiz:[{question:"In NFPA 704, what does the blue section indicate?",options:[{text:"Fire",correct:!1},{text:"Reactivity",correct:!1},{text:"Health",correct:!0},{text:"Special hazards",correct:!1}],explanation:"Blue indicates the health hazard."},{question:"Which section of the SDS covers first aid?",options:[{text:"Section 1",correct:!1},{text:"Section 4",correct:!0},{text:"Section 9",correct:!1},{text:"Section 11",correct:!1}],explanation:"Section 4 is dedicated to first aid measures."},{question:"What must you do before you approach a small chemical spill?",options:[{text:"Start cleaning it up at once",correct:!1},{text:"Identify the substance from the label or SDS and put on the PPE it lists",correct:!0},{text:"Smell it to find out what it is",correct:!1},{text:"Wipe it up with paper towels",correct:!1}],explanation:"First identify the substance from a safe distance and put on the right PPE. If the spill is large, unknown or dangerous, leave, close the door behind you and call 115."},{question:"For general laboratory work with most chemicals, which gloves are the best choice?",options:[{text:"Latex",correct:!1},{text:"Nitrile",correct:!0},{text:"Cotton",correct:!1},{text:"No gloves",correct:!1}],explanation:"Nitrile gloves are a good general choice for most chemicals; for any specific substance, however, the correct type is selected according to the SDS and the chemical resistance chart."}]},{id:3,code:"ELEC-305",icon:"Zap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electrical safety and high voltage",subtitle:"Electrical Safety, Arc Flash & High Voltage",summary:"An analysis of electrical hazards, the effects of electric current on the human body, the arc flash phenomenon, the LOTO standard and Ohm's law as a basis for protection.",blocks:[{body:"Electricity is one of humanity's greatest achievements, but at the same time it is a silent and dangerous force. Every year, many people around the world lose their lives or are injured as a result of electric shock. Most of these incidents occur in factories, on construction projects and even at home, as a result of negligence or lack of knowledge."},{heading:"1. Physical principles and Ohm's law",body:"Ohm's law is the fundamental principle of electrical analysis:",points:["I = V / R (current = voltage ÷ resistance).","Voltage: the force that drives the electrons.","Resistance: a wet body = lower resistance = greater current and greater danger.","Amperage (current): it is the current that kills a person, not voltage alone."]},{heading:"2. Biological effects of electric current",body:"Effects of current on the body (approximate values for 50–60 Hz AC):",points:["1 mA: perception.","10–20 mA: let-go threshold (the person cannot release their grip on the source).","50–100 mA: possible ventricular fibrillation (an irregular heart rhythm).","100+ mA: cardiac arrest and internal burns become more likely."]},{heading:"3. The arc flash phenomenon",body:"An explosive release of energy between conductors:",points:["Heat: can reach approximately 19,400 °C (about four times hotter than the surface of the sun).","Arc blast: an air pressure wave and flying fragments.","Intense radiation and light: eye damage, with temporary or permanent blindness.","Main causes of injury: working on energized equipment, dropped tools and lack of appropriate PPE."]},{heading:"4. The LOTO (lockout/tagout) standard",body:"The safety protocol during maintenance (in accordance with OSHA 1910.147):",points:["Lockout: locking the energy source.","Tagout: a warning tag (name, time, reason).","Zero energy: verifying with a voltmeter that no energy remains."]},{heading:"5. Personal protective equipment (electrical PPE)",body:"Personal protective equipment for electrical work:",points:["Insulating gloves (Class 0/1).","Arc-rated face shield.","Arc-rated (AR) clothing.","Insulating footwear (EH rated)."]},{heading:"6. First aid for electric shock",body:"First aid steps:",points:["First cut off the electrical supply; do not touch the casualty until the power has been disconnected.","Call 122 (ambulance); if there are fallen wires or a fire, also call 115.","If the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR immediately and use an AED.","Cool burns under cool running water for 20 minutes; anyone who has received an electric shock must be examined by a doctor."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_03_loto_steps.mp4",title:"LOTO steps",description:"Locking out and tagging out"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_03_arc_flash.png",title:"Arc flash boundaries",description:"Safety distances"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_03_grounding.mp4",title:"Grounding",description:"The importance of the ground wire"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the lowest level of current that can cause ventricular fibrillation?",options:[{text:"1 mA",correct:!1},{text:"10 mA",correct:!1},{text:"50–100 mA",correct:!0},{text:"5,000 mA",correct:!1}],explanation:"A current of 50–100 milliamperes can cause an irregular heart rhythm (ventricular fibrillation)."},{question:"What is the purpose of LOTO?",options:[{text:"Reducing costs",correct:!1},{text:"Isolating the energy and preventing re-energization",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing the voltage",correct:!1},{text:"Cleaning",correct:!1}],explanation:"LOTO ensures that no energy remains in the equipment during maintenance."},{question:"How does the body's resistance change?",options:[{text:"It always stays the same",correct:!1},{text:"It increases when wet",correct:!1},{text:"It decreases when wet",correct:!0},{text:"There is no relationship",correct:!1}],explanation:"When the skin becomes wet, resistance decreases, and the current and the danger increase."}]},{id:4,code:"MED-401",icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#e11d48",title:"Advanced first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",subtitle:"Advanced First Aid, CPR & Trauma Management",summary:"The DRABCDE protocol, cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) in accordance with the AHA 2025 guidelines, use of the AED, control of life-threatening bleeding (Stop the Bleed) and casualty sorting (triage).",blocks:[{body:'First aid is not only a humanitarian act; it is a precise science that can make the difference between life and death. In emergency care we speak of the "Golden Hour" and the "Platinum Ten Minutes": the sooner a casualty receives treatment, the greater the chance of survival. In every medical emergency, call 122 (ambulance).'},{heading:"1. The DRABCDE protocol",body:"The primary sequence for assessing a casualty:",points:["D — Danger: check for danger to yourself and to the casualty.","R — Response: level of consciousness (AVPU).","A — Airway: open the airway using the head tilt–chin lift or the jaw thrust.","B — Breathing: check for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds; if the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR immediately with chest compressions (the C-A-B sequence).","C — Circulation: check for life-threatening bleeding and signs of shock (health care professionals also check the carotid pulse).","D — Disability: check the level of consciousness, the eyes and the movement of the arms and legs.","E — Exposure: carry out a general examination of the body and cover the casualty to protect against cold."]},{heading:"2. High-quality CPR",body:"AHA 2025 guidelines:",points:["Position: the lower half of the breastbone (sternum).","Depth: 5–6 cm for all adults; approximately 5 cm for children; approximately 4 cm for infants (one third of the depth of the chest). For infants: the two-thumb encircling-hands technique, or the heel of one hand; the two-finger method is no longer recommended.","Rate: 100–120 compressions per minute.","Recoil: after each compression, allow the chest to return fully to its normal position.","Ratio: 30:2 (compressions:breaths); 15:2 for children and infants when two rescuers are present."]},{heading:"3. Using the AED",body:"The automated external defibrillator:",points:["Switch the device on and follow its prompts.",'"Stand clear": make sure that no one is touching the casualty.',"Deliver the shock, then resume CPR immediately."]},{heading:"4. Controlling life-threatening bleeding (Stop the Bleed)",body:"First make sure the scene is safe and call 122, then proceed in this order:",points:["Direct pressure: apply firm, continuous pressure to the wound with both hands. If the wound is deep, pack it with gauze or a clean cloth (wound packing) and maintain the pressure. If the dressing becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; add another dressing on top.","Tourniquet: apply one early for life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg, 5–7 cm above the wound (not over a joint); tighten it until the bleeding stops, write down the time of application and never loosen it.","Shock: lay the casualty on their back, keep them warm and give nothing to eat or drink; in trauma cases do not raise the legs."]},{heading:"5. START triage",body:"Sorting casualties in mass-casualty incidents:",points:["Red (Immediate): life is in danger — first priority.","Yellow (Delayed): serious injuries, but the casualty can wait a short time.","Green (Minor): minor injuries — the casualty can walk.","Black (Expectant/Deceased): not breathing even after the airway has been opened."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_04_cpr_full.mp4",title:"CPR",description:"AHA 2025 guidelines"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_04_jaw_thrust.png",title:"Jaw thrust",description:"For suspected neck and spinal injuries"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_04_triage_sim.mp4",title:"Triage",description:"Sorting casualties"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_04_tourniquet.png",title:"Tourniquet",description:"Position and method of application (5–7 cm above the wound)"}],quiz:[{question:"If a neck injury is suspected, how do you open the airway?",options:[{text:"Head tilt",correct:!1},{text:"Jaw thrust",correct:!0},{text:"Moving the head",correct:!1},{text:"Nothing",correct:!1}],explanation:"The jaw thrust opens the airway without moving the neck."},{question:"What is the ratio of compressions to breaths in adult CPR?",options:[{text:"15:2",correct:!1},{text:"30:2",correct:!0},{text:"100:5",correct:!1},{text:"30:1",correct:!1}],explanation:"The standard for adults is 30 compressions to 2 breaths (15:2 for children and infants when two rescuers are present)."},{question:"In START triage, which color is assigned to a person who is still not breathing after the airway has been opened?",options:[{text:"Red",correct:!1},{text:"Yellow",correct:!1},{text:"Black",correct:!0},{text:"Green",correct:!1}],explanation:"Black (Expectant/Deceased): if the person is still not breathing after the airway has been opened."},{question:"What is the rate of chest compressions in CPR?",options:[{text:"60–80",correct:!1},{text:"80–100",correct:!1},{text:"100–120",correct:!0},{text:"140–160",correct:!1}],explanation:"100–120 compressions per minute."}]},{id:5,code:"STRUC-500",icon:"Building2",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Building safety engineering",subtitle:"Building Safety, Fire Codes & Life Safety Systems",summary:"Engineering codes (IBC/NFPA), fire alarm and suppression systems, fire doors and the design of emergency egress routes.",blocks:[{body:'A building is not only a place to live and work; it must be designed as a "life safety system." Many fire disasters show that engineering deficiencies, such as blocked exits or a lack of fire compartmentation, prevent rapid evacuation and increase the number of victims. Correct design that incorporates alarm systems, automatic suppression and fire-resistant materials can therefore make the difference between life and death. In this module we discuss international codes such as NFPA 101 and the IBC.'},{heading:"1. Building codes and international safety",body:"A code is a set of technical rules that specifies standards for design and construction. Two principal codes are followed in many countries:",points:["IBC (International Building Code): the international building code covering structural aspects, fire protection and life safety (electrical and mechanical systems are regulated by separate codes such as the NEC and the IMC). This code specifies for how many hours the columns and walls of a building must resist fire (fire-resistance rating).","NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code): dedicated to the protection of human life. This standard provides requirements for door widths, corridor lengths and emergency lighting.","Construction types: the IBC divides buildings into five types. Type I, built of concrete and protected steel, has the highest fire resistance, and Type V, built of wood, has the lowest.","Occupant load: the calculation of the maximum number of people who may be in the building at one time, in order to determine how many exits are required and how wide they must be."]},{heading:"2. Fire compartmentation and fire-resistant walls",body:"The aim of this strategy is to divide the building into separate sections (fire compartments) so that fire cannot easily pass from one section to another:",points:["Fire walls: special walls rated by the number of hours of resistance (generally 2 to 4 hours). These walls prevent heat and combustible gases from moving between sections of the building.","Fire doors: fire-resistant doors that must be self-closing and must never be propped open. These doors prevent smoke from entering the stairwells.","Fire dampers: automatic shutters inside air-handling (HVAC) ducts that close by themselves when the temperature rises, so that fire does not spread through the ductwork.","Firestopping: sealing all the openings and gaps in walls and floors through which electrical cables or pipes pass, using fire-resistant materials."]},{heading:"3. Automatic suppression systems",body:"These systems operate without human intervention and bring a fire under rapid control:",points:["Automatic sprinklers: among the most effective systems; according to the NFPA report U.S. Experience with Sprinklers, the death rate in home fires was approximately 89% lower in homes with sprinklers. Only the heads that have been reached by heat operate.","Sprinkler bulbs: the color of the liquid inside the glass bulb indicates the temperature at which it breaks (red = 68 °C, the most common type).","Dry pipe and wet pipe: in very cold areas (such as cold-storage warehouses), a dry pipe system is used so that the water in the pipes does not freeze.","Clean agent systems: the use of gases such as FM-200 (HFC-227ea) or FK-5-1-12 (trade name Novec 1230) in server rooms and museums, so that water does not damage equipment and documents."]},{heading:"4. Fire alarm systems",body:"This is the brain of a building's safety, and its task is to detect fire and alert the occupants (NFPA 72):",points:["FACP (fire alarm control panel): the control center that receives signals from the detectors and issues the commands to sound the alarms and notify the fire service.","Smoke detectors: there are two main types: photoelectric detectors, which are better for slow, smoldering fires, and ionization detectors, which are better for fast, flaming fires.","Notification: the use of loud horns together with flashing lights (strobes) for people with hearing impairments.","Emergency power: during a power outage the system must run on batteries: 24 hours in standby mode followed by 5 minutes in alarm mode (NFPA 72)."]},{heading:"5. Emergency egress routes (means of egress)",body:"Designing the route of escape from any point in a building to the outside requires compliance with the following rules:",points:["Exit access: the route from inside a room to the exit door. There must be no obstructions (chairs, tables, furniture) in these routes.","Exit capacity: the width of doors and stairs must be based on the number of occupants. In general, corridors must be at least 1.12 meters (44 inches) wide, and 0.91 meters (36 inches) is permitted only for spaces with fewer than 50 occupants (IBC).","Direction of travel: exit doors serving 50 or more people (and high-hazard areas) must open outward, in the direction of egress, so that a crowd pressing against the door does not hold it shut. All exit doors must open from the inside without a key.","Exit signs: illuminated exit signs that remain visible in darkness and must be visible from all directions.","Dead-end corridors: the length of a dead-end corridor (one that does not lead to an exit) must generally not exceed 6 meters (20 feet); in some sprinklered buildings it may be longer."]},{heading:"6. High-rise safety",body:"Buildings with an occupied floor more than 23 meters (75 feet) above ground present special hazards and therefore require the following systems:",points:["Smoke control: a stairwell pressurization system that keeps smoke out so that occupants are not asphyxiated.","Fire command center: a dedicated room on the ground floor used by firefighting teams to control the systems of the entire building.","Fire service access elevators: special elevators that only firefighters may use during an emergency.","Areas of refuge: protected areas on upper floors for people with disabilities who cannot descend by the stairs."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_05_sprinkler_test.mp4",title:"Sprinkler test",description:"How the glass bulb breaks under the effect of heat"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_05_egress_plan.png",title:"Evacuation plan",description:"Standard design of egress routes according to NFPA"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_05_fire_damper.mp4",title:"How a fire damper works",description:"Preventing the spread of fire through air-handling ducts"}],quiz:[{question:"According to engineering codes, in which direction must the emergency exit doors of crowded spaces open?",options:[{text:"Inward",correct:!1},{text:"Outward (in the direction of egress)",correct:!0},{text:"Sliding",correct:!1},{text:"It makes no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"They must open outward, in the direction of egress, so that if a crowd presses against the door it opens easily and does not become an obstruction. The door must open from the inside without a key."},{question:"What is the main purpose of a stairwell pressurization system?",options:[{text:"Cooling the building",correct:!1},{text:"Preventing smoke from entering the escape route",correct:!0},{text:"Increasing the lighting",correct:!1},{text:"Extinguishing the fire with air",correct:!1}],explanation:"This system creates a higher air pressure in the stairwell so that smoke cannot enter, and in this way the escape route stays clear."},{question:"What does the red color of a sprinkler's glass bulb indicate?",options:[{text:"A temperature rating of 68 °C",correct:!0},{text:"A temperature rating of 141 °C",correct:!1},{text:"It is only decorative",correct:!1},{text:"It indicates the presence of gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"Red is the most common type; it breaks at 68 °C and sprays water."},{question:"According to the IBC, which construction type has the highest fire resistance?",options:[{text:"Type V (Wood Frame)",correct:!1},{text:"Type III (Ordinary)",correct:!1},{text:"Type I (Fire-Resistive)",correct:!0},{text:"Type IV (Heavy Timber)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Type I is built of concrete and protected steel and is designed to resist a severe fire for several hours without collapsing."}]},{id:6,code:"LAW-600",icon:"Scale",accent:"#475569",title:"Law and legal liability",subtitle:"Legal Responsibilities, Liability & Compliance",summary:"An analysis of legal duties and rights, the penalties for negligence, the civil defense law in Iraq and the responsibility of institutions in relation to disasters.",blocks:[{body:`Safety is not only a moral duty; it is also an important legal obligation. In most countries the law states clearly that building owners, managers, engineers and even ordinary employees bear legal responsibility for ensuring a safe environment. If negligence occurs and causes harm to a person, it can lead to serious legal penalties (civil and criminal), ranging from financial compensation to imprisonment.

In this module we discuss the legal principles of safety, become familiar with the civil defense law of Iraq and learn how individuals can protect themselves and their institutions from legal claims by complying with standards. Note: this module is not legal advice; for any legal question, consult the legal office of the Civil Defense Directorate.`},{heading:"1. The duty of care",body:'The principle of the "duty of care" is one of the main pillars of liability law. Under this principle, any person or party that has control over a place or an activity is responsible for protecting the safety of the people who come to that place or take part in that activity:',points:["Building owners: responsible for providing fire suppression and fire alarm systems and for marking emergency egress routes.","Managers and supervisors: must provide safety training for employees and ensure that personal protective equipment (PPE) is used correctly.","Engineers and technicians: responsible for safe design and for carrying out periodic inspections of all equipment in accordance with approved codes and standards.","Conditions of the duty: for this duty to be established against you, three elements are generally required: 1. the risk was foreseeable; 2. there was a relationship of proximity between you and the injured person; 3. imposing the duty on you is fair, just and reasonable."]},{heading:"2. Negligence and breach of the law",body:"Negligence is the situation in which a person fails to fulfill their legal duty of protection and, as a result, another person suffers harm. Under common law, four elements are required to prove negligence in court. Iraqi civil law expresses the same principle through three elements: fault (transgression), damage and the causal link between them:",points:["1. Duty: it must be shown that you owed a legal duty to that person.","2. Breach of duty: you failed to do what was required (for example, you did not inspect the fire extinguishers).","3. Harm (damage): actual harm must have occurred (physical, material or psychological).","4. Causation: the breach of your duty must have directly caused that harm (for example, if you had repaired the fire alarm system, the injured person would have been able to escape).","Legal defenses: the accused party may defend themselves by proving that the injured person had voluntarily accepted the risk (assumption of risk), or that the harm resulted from an unforeseeable cause beyond human control (force majeure)."]},{heading:"3. The civil defense law in Iraq and the Kurdistan Region",body:"The legal framework for safety is regulated by several laws and official regulations:",points:["Iraqi Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013: the principal civil defense law in Iraq, which replaced the former Law No. 64 of 1978. This law assigns to the General Directorate of Civil Defense the duty of inspecting premises and monitoring compliance with safety measures. Its application in the Kurdistan Region should be confirmed with the Directorate's legal office.","Approval of building plans: under the law, municipalities send building plans to the Civil Defense so that fire prevention and fire suppression measures can be reviewed before a construction permit is issued.","Inspections and penalties: the Duhok Civil Defense Directorate inspects residential and commercial projects; violations can lead to the closure of the premises and to fines or imprisonment in accordance with the law.","Safety permit: under the law, Civil Defense approval is required before a license is granted for any commercial or industrial activity (particularly the storage and use of hazardous materials)."]},{heading:"4. Corporate liability",body:'A company, as a "legal person," is responsible for incidents that occur on its projects:',points:["Vicarious liability: the company is responsible for the mistakes of its employees if they occur during working hours and in the course of their official duties.","Direct liability: if the company has not provided employees with the necessary training, or has used old and unsafe equipment, it is directly liable.","Financial losses: in addition to compensating the injured, the company suffers damage to its reputation and higher insurance premiums."]},{heading:"5. Legal protection strategies",body:"To protect life and to ensure legal protection, engineers and managers must take the following measures:",points:['Documentation: the golden rule says: "If it was not written down, it did not happen." Record every inspection, repair and training session with the date and a signature.',"Continuous training: ensure that all employees know the types of fire extinguishers and the evacuation protocol.","Internal inspections: before government inspection teams arrive, carry out periodic inspections yourself to identify safety deficiencies.","Formal contracts: between the owner and the contractors, the safety responsibilities of each party must be clearly set out in writing."]},{heading:"6. Ethics and professional responsibility",body:"Beyond the law, engineers have an ethical duty that must not be forgotten:",points:["Engineering ethics: protecting human life comes before financial profit. Never accept decisions that put safety at risk, even if management puts pressure on you.","Reporting: it is your ethical duty to report clearly any existing hazard before a disaster occurs."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_06_negligence_chart.png",title:"The negligence cycle",description:"The four legal elements required to prove negligence in court"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_06_legal_brief.mp4",title:"Legal liability",description:"An analysis of a real fire disaster case under Iraqi law"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_06_documentation_log.png",title:"Documentation example",description:"How to record safety inspections for legal protection"}],quiz:[{question:"Which of the following elements is NOT a requirement for proving negligence in court?",options:[{text:"Existence of a duty",correct:!1},{text:"Criminal intent",correct:!0},{text:"Harm",correct:!1},{text:"Causation (causal link)",correct:!1}],explanation:"Negligence does not require criminal intent; even if you did not cause harm deliberately, if you failed to fulfill your duty of protection you are legally liable."},{question:'What is meant by "vicarious liability"?',options:[{text:"The personal liability of an employee",correct:!1},{text:"The liability of an institution for the actions of its employees",correct:!0},{text:"Firefighting law",correct:!1},{text:"Environmental compensation",correct:!1}],explanation:"This term means that an institution or company is responsible for the actions and mistakes of its employees while they are carrying out their duties."},{question:"In Iraq, which law regulates civil defense and the safety measures for institutions and buildings?",options:[{text:"Law No. 64 of 1978",correct:!1},{text:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013",correct:!0},{text:"Law No. 7 of 2005",correct:!1},{text:"The Traffic Law",correct:!1}],explanation:"Civil Defense Law No. 44 of 2013 is the principal civil defense law in Iraq; Law No. 64 of 1978 was repealed by this law. Confirm its application in the Kurdistan Region with the Directorate's legal office."},{question:'Complete the golden rule of legal protection: "If it was not written down..."',options:[{text:"It is not important",correct:!1},{text:"It will be done later",correct:!1},{text:"It did not actually happen",correct:!0},{text:"It is legal",correct:!1}],explanation:"In court, records and written documents are the strongest evidence; any work that has not been recorded is treated as if it had never been done."}]},{id:7,code:"PSYCH-700",icon:"BrainCircuit",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Disaster psychology and human behavior",subtitle:"Disaster Psychology, Crowd Dynamics & Crisis Leadership",summary:"An analysis of human biological and psychological responses during disasters, crowd management and the principles of emergency leadership.",blocks:[{body:"In major disasters, whether fire, earthquake or explosion, human behavior itself can become a major cause of casualties: hesitation, crowding at a single door and crowd pressure on the exits. For example, in the fire at The Station nightclub in the United States (2003), 100 people died; according to the NIST investigation, most people tried to leave through the main entrance by which they had come in, and crowding and blockage occurred there, even though other exits were available. Understanding crowd psychology is a core duty of every civil defense leader in order to manage evacuation rapidly and reduce casualties."},{heading:"1. The biological response to danger (fight-flight-freeze)",body:"When the brain perceives a threat to life, the sympathetic nervous system is activated and the body releases the hormones adrenaline and cortisol, which produce several types of response:",points:["Fight: the body prepares to defend itself. In disasters, this can lead to aggressive behavior toward others in order to get through.","Flight: an attempt to escape rapidly. This can cause a crowd to surge in a single direction.","Freeze: the person is unable to move or make a decision and stays in place like a statue. This is one of the most dangerous states, because the person remains in danger.","Fawn (appeasement): the person tries to placate the source of the threat, or follows the group without thinking.",'Changes in the brain: under fear, the amygdala takes control and the prefrontal cortex, which is responsible for rational decision-making, is weakened. This leads to "tunnel vision": seeing only one goal and ignoring other exits.']},{heading:"2. Crowd psychology and herd behavior",body:"In emergencies, people often imitate the majority without thinking, and this can lead to disaster. Note: disaster research shows that uncontrolled panic is less common than people assume; most people behave rationally, but a lack of information and guidance creates hesitation and crowding:",points:["Herd behavior: if one person runs, many others will run after them even if they do not know why. This spreads fear like a virus.","Funnel effect: everyone converging on a single door (often the door by which they entered), which leads to people being asphyxiated and trampled.","Panic contagion: the rapid spread of fear through a crowd, which weakens the authority of the leadership.","Deindividuation: when a person becomes part of a large group, their sense of personal responsibility decreases and they may behave in uncontrolled ways."]},{heading:"3. Crisis leadership",body:"Leadership during disasters requires calmness and direct commands in order to prevent panic:",points:["Calm presence: the leader must speak and act calmly; if the leader shows fear, the people will become afraid.",'Short, clear commands: instead of long speeches, use commands such as "Move to the right," "Stop" and "Follow me."',"Hand signals: when there is loud noise, use clear hand signals to direct people.","Isolating panickers: people who are screaming and spreading fear should be gently separated from the group so that they do not affect others."]},{heading:"4. Psychological first aid (PFA)",body:"The RAPID model (from Johns Hopkins University) is used to reduce psychological trauma among victims: R reflective listening, A assessment of needs, P prioritization, I intervention, D disposition (referral to the necessary services). Key points:",points:["R - Reflective listening: listen and reflect the person's feelings back to them; let the victim express their concerns without being judged.","A - Assessment: assess immediate needs (water, food, contact with family).","Safety and comfort: provide a calm place away from the scenes of the disaster.",'What not to do: never say "Everything will be fine" or "I know what you are going through," because these are unfounded promises and have a negative effect.']},{heading:"5. Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)",body:"Post-traumatic stress disorder is a risk not only for victims but also for emergency response teams:",points:["Symptoms: nightmares, flashbacks (re-experiencing scenes of the incident) and hypervigilance.","Duration: if the symptoms persist for more than a month or disrupt daily life, help from mental health professionals is needed.","Resilience: psychological preparation before an incident, operational debriefing after missions and peer support. According to WHO guidance, compulsory single-session psychological debriefing is not recommended for preventing PTSD."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_07_crowd_dynamics.mp4",title:"Crowd dynamics",description:"An analysis of how people move under fear"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_07_pfa_model.png",title:"The RAPID model",description:"The steps of psychological first aid"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_07_leadership_drill.mp4",title:"Leadership training",description:"How to direct people by voice and hand signals"}],quiz:[{question:'What is meant by "freeze" in the biological response to fear?',options:[{text:"Escaping rapidly",correct:!1},{text:"Fighting to protect one's life",correct:!1},{text:"Freezing and staying in place without moving",correct:!0},{text:"Conforming to the group",correct:!1}],explanation:"Freeze is a psychological state in which a person cannot make any decision or move, and this increases the risk of remaining trapped in the disaster."},{question:"According to the PFA protocol, which behavior is WRONG when helping a psychologically traumatized person?",options:[{text:"Listening calmly",correct:!1},{text:"Providing water and food",correct:!1},{text:'Saying "Everything will be fine"',correct:!0},{text:"Contacting their family",correct:!1}],explanation:`Making unfounded promises such as "It will be fine" has a negative effect and reduces the victim's trust.`},{question:"What is the best way for a civil defense leader to give commands during a crisis?",options:[{text:"Shouting and showing fear",correct:!1},{text:"Short, clear commands in a calm voice",correct:!0},{text:"Ignoring the people",correct:!1},{text:"Explaining the chemical details of the disaster",correct:!1}],explanation:"People need direct and clear guidance; the leader's calm reduces fear among the people."},{question:'How does the phenomenon of "herd behavior" work?',options:[{text:"Individual thinking",correct:!1},{text:"Imitating the behavior of the majority without thinking",correct:!0},{text:"Following the rules",correct:!1},{text:"Rescuing others",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under fear, people are more inclined to follow others without considering whether that route is correct."}]},{id:8,code:"ENV-800",icon:"Leaf",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Environmental safety and waste management",subtitle:"Environmental Safety & Waste Management",summary:"The environmental impact of fire and chemicals, the correct management of medical waste, the protection of water sources and environmental responsibility.",blocks:[{body:"When we speak about safety, we must consider not only human safety but also the safety of the environment and of nature. Every disaster or safety incident (such as a fire or a chemical spill) leaves a long-term impact on the environment: smoke pollutes the air, hazardous waste is generated and firefighting water becomes toxic and mixes with groundwater. In this module we discuss waste management, the protection of water and air and the environmental responsibility of institutions in order to protect future generations."},{heading:"1. Medical and biological waste",body:"Hospitals, clinics and laboratories generate special types of waste that can transmit bacteria and viruses. This waste must not be discarded like ordinary garbage; it must be segregated by color. The colors differ from country to country; the World Health Organization (WHO) system is used here, and the hospital's internal guidelines must always be followed:",points:["Sharps (yellow): all items that can puncture the skin, such as needles, surgical blades and broken glass. They must be collected in rigid, puncture-resistant plastic containers (sharps containers).","Infectious/biohazard waste (yellow under WHO; red in some countries, such as the United States): items contaminated with blood or body tissue, and laboratory materials. It must be treated by autoclaving or destroyed by complete incineration.","General waste (black): clean paper, cardboard and plastics that carry no biological risk.","Pharmaceutical and chemical waste (brown under WHO): expired medicines or laboratory chemical waste, which must be treated by special procedures."]},{heading:"2. Water pollution from firefighting (fire runoff)",body:'The water used to extinguish large fires is not merely dirty water; it is a "liquid poison" full of chemicals and metals:',points:['Pollutants: it contains PAHs from burning plastics, heavy metals (such as lead and mercury) and hazardous firefighting foam substances such as PFAS, which are called "forever chemicals" because they do not break down in the environment.',"Containment: safety engineers must build temporary barriers so that this toxic water does not enter sewers or rivers.","Water treatment: this water must be collected and sent to dedicated wastewater treatment plants before it is discharged."]},{heading:"3. Air pollution and smoke chemistry",body:"Large fires, particularly industrial fires and wildfires, release highly hazardous particles into the air:",points:["Fine particulate matter (PM2.5): very small particles that penetrate deep into the lungs and cause heart and lung disease and cancer.","Toxic gases: such as carbon monoxide (CO) and hydrogen cyanide (HCN), which are produced when buildings burn and which poison the surrounding air.","Acid rain: gases such as SOx and NOx combine with rain and damage vegetation and forests."]},{heading:"4. Electronic waste (e-waste)",body:"Broken or burned electronic devices (mobile phones, computers) are a major environmental hazard:",points:["Toxic metals: they contain lead, mercury and cadmium, which, if they enter water or soil, do not break down, persist for a very long time and endanger human health.","Recycling: these devices must not be discarded in public places; they must be sent to dedicated recycling facilities so that valuable metals (gold and silver) can be recovered."]},{heading:"5. Environmental responsibility and the ISO 14001 standard",body:"Every institution should have an environmental management system to reduce harm:",points:["The 3R principle: Reduce, Reuse and Recycle.","ISO 14001: a voluntary international standard for environmental management systems (EMS); institutions that adopt it establish an environmental policy, measurable objectives and periodic internal audits.","Monitoring and inspection: carrying out periodic testing of the soil and water around factories to confirm that no chemicals are being released."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_08_waste_colors.png",title:"The color system",description:"Segregating medical waste by color according to WHO"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_08_hazmat_disposal.mp4",title:"Disposal of hazardous materials",description:"Correct procedures for disposing of chemical waste"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_08_runoff_control.png",title:"Controlling firefighting water",description:"How to prevent toxic water from mixing with river water"}],quiz:[{question:"Under the WHO system, in which container are needles and medical blades discarded?",options:[{text:"Red bag (biohazard)",correct:!1},{text:"Black bag (general)",correct:!1},{text:"Rigid yellow container (sharps container)",correct:!0},{text:"Blue bag",correct:!1}],explanation:"All sharp items must be discarded in a rigid yellow container (sharps container) so that they do not cause injuries or transmit disease."},{question:"What is the main danger of firefighting water (fire runoff)?",options:[{text:"It is only dirty",correct:!1},{text:"It carries toxic substances and heavy metals into the environment",correct:!0},{text:"Its temperature is very high",correct:!1},{text:"There is no danger",correct:!1}],explanation:"This water carries poisons and burned chemicals with it and can contaminate surface water and groundwater."},{question:"What danger do PM2.5 particles in fire smoke pose to human health?",options:[{text:"They only change the color of the skin",correct:!1},{text:"They penetrate deep into the lungs and cause serious diseases",correct:!0},{text:"They cause no harm",correct:!1},{text:"They are good for breathing",correct:!1}],explanation:"PM2.5 are very small particles that penetrate deep into the lungs and cause long-term problems of the heart and respiratory system."},{question:'What is meant by "Recycle" in the 3R principle?',options:[{text:"Burning waste",correct:!1},{text:"Recovering waste and reprocessing it into new products",correct:!0},{text:"Reducing costs",correct:!1},{text:"Dumping waste outside the city",correct:!1}],explanation:"Recycling means converting waste into new materials in order to protect the environment."}]},{id:9,code:"MGMT-900",icon:"Network",accent:"#2563eb",title:"The Incident Command System (ICS)",subtitle:"Incident Command System & Strategic Management",summary:"The standard Incident Command System (ICS) for managing incidents and disasters, interagency coordination and resource management.",blocks:[{body:"When a major disaster occurs, such as a flood, an earthquake or an industrial fire, the biggest problem is often not only a shortage of resources but a lack of coordination and organization. Dozens of different teams (firefighting, police, ambulance and volunteers) frequently arrive at the scene at the same time; without an organized system, this leads to confusion and duplication of effort. The Incident Command System (ICS) is the solution to this problem. The system was developed in the 1970s in the United States (the FIRESCOPE program in California); today it is part of the U.S. National Incident Management System, NIMS (FEMA), and has been adopted by many countries and organizations to manage crises in an organized way."},{heading:"1. The five major functions of ICS",body:"ICS works on the principle of dividing responsibilities. Every incident, whether small or large, requires these five functions (for certain incidents, an Intelligence/Investigations function can be added as a sixth function):",points:["1. Incident Commander (IC): the commander of the incident; a single person who makes the final decisions and is responsible for setting the main objectives and for the overall safety of the teams (in multi-agency incidents, Unified Command is used).","2. Operations: the section that carries out the direct work in the field (such as fire suppression, rescue or medical treatment).","3. Planning: collecting information about the incident, analyzing data and writing the Incident Action Plan (IAP) for the upcoming operational periods.","4. Logistics: meeting all needs (food, water, fuel, communications equipment and accommodation for the teams). Without logistics, field teams cannot sustain their work.","5. Finance/Administration: managing the financial side, recording working hours and equipment costs and handling compensation claims."]},{heading:"2. Span of control",body:"One of the most important principles of ICS is that no supervisor should be overloaded. This principle defines how many people a supervisor can effectively control:",points:["Appropriate number: according to NIMS, each supervisor should control 3 to 7 people or units, and the recommended ratio is 1 to 5.","Why 5? If the number exceeds 7, the supervisor loses control and errors occur; if it is fewer than 3, resources are wasted.","Modular organization: ICS expands in modules; whenever necessary, new units are activated so that the span of control remains within an appropriate range."]},{heading:"3. Common language and standard terminology",body:"In an emergency, a misunderstanding can cost lives. ICS requires all parties to communicate in a common, clear language:",points:["Plain language: ten-codes (such as 10-4) and agency-specific codes are not used; they are a legacy system and their meaning differs from one agency to another. Simple, clear language must be used so that all agencies (police, firefighting, ambulance) understand.",'Standard position titles: the names of positions and units must be the same at every incident (for example, Incident Commander, not "chief" or "person in charge" at one incident and some other title at another).',"Geographic division: dividing the incident scene into divisions in order to control each area separately, and into groups according to function."]},{heading:"4. The Incident Action Plan (IAP)",body:"The IAP is a written document that sets out the course of action for each operational period:",points:["Content: the plan states: What is the objective? Who is responsible? Which radio channel is to be used? And where is the medical post?","Plan duration: the plan is usually written for the next 12 to 24 hours.","SMART objectives: objectives must be specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound (for example: extinguish the fire in the northern warehouse within 4 hours)."]},{heading:"5. Unified Command",body:"When an incident affects several agencies or several administrative jurisdictions, Unified Command is used:",points:["Coordination: instead of each agency working independently, the officials of all agencies gather in one place and agree on a single plan.","Benefit: it prevents conflicts and ensures that the same task is not carried out twice by two teams.","ICP (Incident Command Post): a designated physical location where the incident commanders gather to manage the incident."]},{heading:"6. Demobilization",body:"Ending an operation in an organized way is as important as starting it:",points:["Demobilization plan: teams must be released in an orderly sequence so that no gaps are left in the field and all personnel and equipment are accounted for.","After-Action Review (AAR): holding an evaluation meeting after the incident to identify strengths and weaknesses, so that future operations improve."]}],media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_09_ics_structure.png",title:"ICS organizational structure",description:"A diagram of the five major functions of incident command"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_09_command_post.mp4",title:"Incident Command Post (ICP)",description:"How field coordination is managed during disasters"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_09_span_control.png",title:"Span of control",description:"An explanation of the principle of 1 supervisor for 5 personnel"}],quiz:[{question:"According to ICS/NIMS, what is the recommended span of control?",options:[{text:"10 people",correct:!1},{text:"5 people",correct:!0},{text:"2 people",correct:!1},{text:"15 people",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to NIMS, the span of control is between 3 and 7, and a ratio of 1 to 5 is recommended so that the supervisor can monitor the team closely."},{question:"Which section is responsible for providing food, water and communications equipment to the teams?",options:[{text:"Operations",correct:!1},{text:"Planning",correct:!1},{text:"Logistics",correct:!0},{text:"Finance",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Logistics Section is responsible for meeting all physical needs and providing services."},{question:'What is the main purpose of "plain language" in communications?',options:[{text:"So that people do not understand",correct:!1},{text:"To ensure error-free coordination among all agencies",correct:!0},{text:"To reduce working hours",correct:!1},{text:"To make speech sound better",correct:!1}],explanation:"Using simple, clear language (not codes) allows the police, firefighting and ambulance services to understand one another correctly."},{question:"When is Unified Command used?",options:[{text:"When the incident is small",correct:!1},{text:"When only one agency is at the scene",correct:!1},{text:"When several agencies or several administrative jurisdictions are involved in the incident",correct:!0},{text:"It is never used",correct:!1}],explanation:"When several agencies or several administrative jurisdictions are involved in the incident, Unified Command is established so that all parties work to a single plan."}]},{id:10,code:"TECH-1000",icon:"Satellite",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Future technology and innovation",subtitle:"Future Technology in Emergency Response",summary:"The role of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs), firefighting robots, thermal imaging cameras, artificial intelligence (AI) and IoT systems in rescue operations.",blocks:[{body:"Over the last ten years, technology has markedly changed the way civil defense and rescue work is carried out. New devices and smart sensors can reach places that people can access only with difficulty, allowing detailed information on hazards to be collected and help to arrive faster. In this module we discuss the technologies currently being developed to become part of civil protection systems around the world."},{heading:"1. Unmanned aerial vehicles (drones / UAVs)",body:"The drone is one of the greatest innovations in the emergency field. These devices can carry out difficult field tasks without putting the lives of rescue teams at risk:",points:["Aerial surveillance: drones can send real-time images and video to the command center so that the overall picture of the disaster becomes clear.","Thermal imaging: locating living people in forests, at night or in thick smoke by detecting body heat.","Emergency delivery: sending medicines, medical supplies or communications equipment to places cut off by floods or earthquakes.","Three-dimensional (3D) mapping: drones can produce a 3D map of the incident scene in a short time so that rescue and evacuation plans can be prepared more effectively."]},{heading:"2. Firefighting and technical rescue robots",body:"Robots act as a support force in places where the temperature or toxic gases exceed human capabilities:",points:["Firefighting robots: large remote-controlled machines that can approach the heart of a fire and spray water at high pressure (such as the Colossus robot).","Snake robots: slender robots that can enter the voids of collapsed buildings to locate casualties.","Exoskeletons: powered mechanical suits that increase firefighters' physical capabilities for carrying heavy loads with less fatigue.","HAZMAT robots: specialized robots for handling nuclear and chemical materials, so that people are not exposed to poisoning."]},{heading:"3. Artificial intelligence and big data analysis (AI & big data)",body:"Artificial intelligence can analyze millions of data points in a few seconds:",points:["Hazard prediction: analyzing weather and sensor data to predict the risk of floods or wildfires before they occur.","Intelligent response systems: AI can assist call-takers in prioritizing emergency calls (115) by urgency and dispatching the appropriate team; the final decision remains in human hands.","Computer vision: automatically detecting fire or smoke through city surveillance cameras and issuing rapid alerts."]},{heading:"4. The Internet of Things (IoT) and smart buildings",body:"The Internet of Things (IoT) connects devices to a network so that a building becomes an intelligent system:",points:["Smart buildings: sensors can automatically close fire doors and shut down the air-handling system so that smoke does not spread.","Wearables: smartwatches for emergency teams that transmit oxygen levels, heart rate and location (GPS) to the control center.","Connected vehicles: fire engines that can automatically turn traffic lights green (traffic signal preemption) so that they reach their destination faster."]},{heading:"5. Virtual reality (VR) and augmented reality (AR)",body:"Using visual technology for training and field operations:",points:["VR training: allows rescue teams to train on major disaster scenarios without any risk.","AR inside helmets: some new firefighting helmets contain a small display that shows the building plan and the air level in the SCBA cylinder while in smoke."]},{heading:"6. Nanotechnology and new materials",body:"Developing the equipment of the future for body protection:",points:["Nanotechnology: producing firefighting clothing that is lighter but more resistant to heat.","New batteries: sodium-ion or solid-state batteries, which carry a lower risk of ignition and explosion than ordinary lithium-ion batteries."]},{heading:"7. Challenges and ethical aspects",body:"Despite its great benefits, technology raises a number of important questions:",points:["Cost: the high price of these devices is an obstacle to their adoption by all countries.","Data protection: the risk of hacking into smart building systems or command systems.","AI decisions: is it right for artificial intelligence, instead of humans, to make decisions about human lives when sorting casualties (triage)?"]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_10_drone_thermal.mp4",title:"Drones and locating casualties",description:"How thermal cameras are used to locate people in forests"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_10_smart_helmet.jpg",title:"The smart AR helmet",description:"Displaying environmental information on the firefighter's helmet screen"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_10_robot_extinguish.mp4",title:"A firefighting robot in action",description:"Extinguishing a factory fire by remote control"}],quiz:[{question:"How do thermal imaging cameras on drones help rescue teams?",options:[{text:"For taking colorful photographs",correct:!1},{text:"For detecting the human body by its heat in darkness and smoke",correct:!0},{text:"For extinguishing fires",correct:!1},{text:"For connecting to mobile phones",correct:!1}],explanation:"The human body is warmer than the surrounding environment, so thermal cameras can clearly show where people are, particularly at night and in smoke. Their ability to detect people beneath stone and concrete is limited."},{question:"What is the main benefit of exoskeletons for civil defense personnel?",options:[{text:"Flying through the air",correct:!1},{text:"Increasing physical capability for carrying heavy loads and reducing fatigue",correct:!0},{text:"Purifying water",correct:!1},{text:"Chemical research",correct:!1}],explanation:"These mechanical suits add power to human muscles so that the wearer can carry casualties or heavy equipment more easily."},{question:"In the field of safety, what is meant by a smart building?",options:[{text:"A beautiful building",correct:!1},{text:"A building whose sensors and devices are networked to respond automatically to hazards",correct:!0},{text:"A building made entirely of glass",correct:!1},{text:"A building without electricity",correct:!1}],explanation:"In smart buildings, the system itself detects the hazard and, without human intervention, closes doors, shuts down the air-handling system and alerts the teams."},{question:"How can artificial intelligence (AI) predict disasters?",options:[{text:"By magic",correct:!1},{text:"By analyzing large volumes of weather data and data from ground sensors",correct:!0},{text:"By telephoning people",correct:!1},{text:"It cannot make predictions",correct:!1}],explanation:"AI relies on data from past years and on recent changes to identify where the probability of a hazard is highest; a prediction is a probability, not a certainty."}]},{id:11,code:"QUAKE-1100",icon:"Activity",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Seismology and safety engineering",subtitle:"Seismology & Earthquake Resistant Design",summary:"A scientific analysis of tectonic plates, seismic waves, soil liquefaction, earthquake-resistant building design and the Drop, Cover, Hold On procedure during shaking.",blocks:[{body:`Iraq and the Kurdistan Region lie in a seismically active zone, particularly the northern and eastern areas close to the Zagros mountain range. This zone was formed by the collision of the Arabian Plate with the Eurasian Plate. In this module we discuss, at an academic level, the physics of earthquakes and the engineering measures that protect lives and buildings.

Personal protection during shaking (Drop, Cover, Hold On): drop to the ground where you are (Drop), protect your head and neck under a sturdy table (Cover) and hold on to the table leg until the shaking stops (Hold On). Stay away from windows and heavy equipment. Do not go under a doorway and do not rely on the "triangle of life"; these are not approved procedures. Do not run outside or use the elevator during the shaking. After the shaking, shut off the gas only if you smell gas or hear a leak.`},{heading:"1. The mechanism of earthquakes and tectonic plates",body:"An earthquake is a complex physical process explained by the elastic rebound theory:",points:["Tectonic plates: the Earth's crust consists of several large pieces that move over a softer layer. The Arabian Plate moves approximately two centimeters per year toward the northeast.","Hypocenter and epicenter: the hypocenter is the point beneath the ground where the energy is released. The epicenter is the point directly above it on the Earth's surface, near which the greatest destruction often occurs.","Faults: knowledge of fault systems such as the Zagros Fold-Thrust Belt is essential for civil engineers when designing projects."]},{heading:"2. Analysis of seismic waves",body:"Earthquake energy propagates in the form of waves, which are divided into three main types:",points:["P-waves (primary): the fastest waves; they travel as a push through all materials (solid, liquid and gas). These are the first waves recorded by seismographs.","S-waves (secondary): slower, but their shaking is stronger. They travel only through solid materials, their motion is perpendicular to the direction of travel (side to side or up and down) and they cause major damage to buildings.","Surface waves: they travel along the Earth's surface, last the longest and are the main cause of large-scale destruction in cities."]},{heading:"3. Scientific scales and soil liquefaction",body:"To describe the strength of an earthquake, a distinction must be made between magnitude and intensity:",points:["Magnitude scale: measures the energy released. Today the moment magnitude scale (Mw) is used, which has replaced the Richter scale. Each additional unit means 10 times the wave amplitude on the seismograph and approximately 32 times more energy.","Modified Mercalli Intensity scale: measures the intensity of shaking at each location according to its effects on people and buildings.","Soil liquefaction: in areas of wet, sandy soil, shaking causes the soil to lose its solid properties and behave like a liquid, which makes buildings sink or overturn."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_11_seismic_waves.mp4",title:"The physics of waves",description:"An analysis of how P-waves and S-waves travel through the Earth"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_11_building_design.png",title:"Earthquake-resistant engineering",description:"The base isolation technique for protecting buildings"}],quiz:[{question:"Which type of wave is the fastest and reaches seismographic stations first?",options:[{text:"S-waves",correct:!1},{text:"P-waves",correct:!0},{text:"Surface waves",correct:!1},{text:"Love waves",correct:!1}],explanation:"P-waves (primary) are the fastest waves and travel through all types of material."}]},{id:12,code:"PLAN-1200",icon:"ClipboardList",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Emergency planning and preparedness",subtitle:"Emergency Planning & Family Preparedness",summary:"Preparing an emergency evacuation plan for the family and the workplace, and assembling the basic supplies needed for survival.",blocks:[{body:"Planning before any disaster is the best way to reduce fear and protect life. In this module we will learn how to prepare a scientific plan for families and institutions that covers every possible hazard."},{heading:"1. The family emergency plan",body:"Every family needs to agree on a clear plan:",points:["Escape routes: identify two routes out of every room in the house, so that if one is blocked, the other can be used.","Assembly points: designate two places, one near the house and one outside the neighborhood in case returning home is not possible.","Out-of-area contact: keep the number of a relative who lives outside the city, so that they can serve as the point of contact between family members."]},{heading:"2. The emergency kit",body:"Essential supplies must be ready for at least 72 hours (3 days):",points:["Water and food: about 4 liters of water per person per day, for at least 3 days, together with non-perishable food such as canned goods.","Light and communication: a lantern or flashlight, spare batteries and a battery-powered radio for listening to official information.","Medical needs: family members' specific medications and a first aid kit."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_12_plan_making.mp4",title:"How to prepare the plan",description:"The steps for drawing an evacuation map of the building"}],quiz:[{question:"For at least how many days should an emergency kit cover the family's needs?",options:[{text:"1 day",correct:!1},{text:"3 days (72 hours)",correct:!0},{text:"1 week",correct:!1},{text:"1 month",correct:!1}],explanation:"Preparedness guidance (such as Ready.gov and FEMA) recommends preparing supplies for at least 72 hours (3 days), because at the start of a major disaster help may arrive late."}]},{id:13,code:"FLOOD-1300",icon:"Waves",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Floods and water safety procedures",subtitle:"Flood Dynamics & Water Rescue",summary:"Understanding the types of flood, vehicle safety and the principles of emergency rescue in water.",blocks:[{body:"Floods are one of the most widespread disasters in the Kurdistan Region. Just a few centimeters of moving water can cause major disasters. In this module we discuss how to protect lives and vehicles when water levels rise. Never walk or drive into flood water, and call 115."},{heading:"1. Types of floods and their hazards",body:"Floods are classified by their speed and location:",points:["Flash flood: occurs within 6 hours of heavy rainfall, and is the most dangerous type because there is very little time to prepare.","Danger to vehicles: just 30 cm of moving water is enough to lift and carry away most vehicles.","Danger to people: 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down and cause drowning."]},{heading:"2. Rescue procedures: Reach, Throw, Row, Go",body:"If you see a person in the water, first call 115, then carry out these steps in order:",points:["REACH: reach the person with a pole or another long object without entering the water yourself.","THROW: throw them something that floats (such as a tire, or a rope tied to a light floating object).","ROW: if a boat and training are available, use them to approach safely.","GO: only specialist teams may enter the water; never enter flood water as a volunteer."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_13_water_safety.mp4",title:"Flood protection",description:"Correct procedures for dealing with the force of water"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the minimum depth of moving water that can lift and carry away an ordinary car?",options:[{text:"10 cm",correct:!1},{text:"30 cm",correct:!0},{text:"100 cm",correct:!1},{text:"5 cm",correct:!1}],explanation:"30 centimeters (12 inches) of moving water is enough to lift and carry away most cars; even shallower water can cause the driver to lose control of the vehicle."}]},{id:14,code:"GAS-1400",icon:"Fuel",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Gas safety and the physics of explosions",subtitle:"Natural Gas & LPG Advanced Safety",summary:"An analysis of gas density relative to air, the explosive limits (LEL/UEL) and civil defense strategies for controlling gas leaks.",blocks:[{body:"Fuel gas is a major source of energy in industry and in homes, but the smallest mistake can turn it into a devastating bomb. In this module we discuss gas dynamics: from vapor density to autoignition temperature. Understanding this information is essential for every safety engineer in order to design protection systems in accordance with NFPA standards."},{heading:"1. Gas physics and vapor density",body:"To prevent gas from accumulating, you must know whether the gas is lighter or heavier than air (air = 1):",points:["Natural gas (methane): lighter than air (approximately 0.55). If there is a leak, it rises and accumulates beneath the ceiling; high-level ventilation is therefore required.","LPG (propane/butane): heavier than air (approximately 1.5 to 2). When it leaks, it collects like water on the floor and in low places (basements, trenches). This is very dangerous because it can reach sparks from switches and low-mounted appliances.","Gas odorization (mercaptan): pure gas is odorless, so companies add mercaptan compounds (such as ethyl mercaptan) so that people can detect the danger by smell."]},{heading:"2. Explosive limits (flammability limits, LEL/UEL)",body:"A gas explodes only when its concentration in air lies between two limits:",points:["Lower explosive limit (LEL): the lowest concentration of gas in air that can ignite. If the gas concentration is below this, no explosion occurs.","Upper explosive limit (UEL): the highest concentration of gas; if the gas concentration is above this, there is not enough oxygen and no explosion occurs (but when it mixes with air and falls between the two limits, it can ignite).","Asphyxiation hazard: gas accumulating in enclosed spaces displaces oxygen and can cause a silent death (asphyxiation)."]},{heading:"3. Technical emergency procedures",body:"When a gas leak is suspected, the Civil Defense follows these rules:",points:["No sources of ignition: operating an electrical switch, using a telephone or any electrical device inside the premises can provide the energy needed for an explosion, as can any open flame.","Actions: open doors and windows (without switching on electric fans), close the main valve if it is safe to do so, evacuate people quietly and call 115 from outside the premises."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_14_gas_physics.mp4",title:"Gas explosion dynamics",description:"A laboratory analysis of the LEL and UEL"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_14_gas_detectors.png",title:"Industrial detectors",description:"Types of gas leak detection devices in factories"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does LPG accumulate at ground level when it leaks?",options:[{text:"Because it is cold",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is heavier than air",correct:!0},{text:"Because it has a strong smell",correct:!1},{text:"Because it is natural gas",correct:!1}],explanation:"LPG is heavier than air (vapor density > 1), so it sinks and accumulates in low places."}]},{id:15,code:"PLAN-1500",icon:"Landmark",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Strategic emergency planning",subtitle:"Institutional Emergency & Continuity Planning",summary:"Preparing evacuation plans for government institutions, managing crises and ensuring business continuity.",blocks:[{body:"Planning before a disaster makes the difference between success and catastrophe. This module has been prepared for civil defense managers and officers so that they know how to develop an emergency response plan (ERP) for government and commercial buildings."},{heading:"1. The core elements of an ERP",body:"Every good plan needs to contain these three components:",points:["Evacuation strategy: mapping the escape routes and ensuring that there are no obstructions in the path of occupants and emergency teams.","Assembly points: designating safe locations outside the building, away from the building and from the access routes of emergency vehicles, that have been approved through an engineering inspection.","Chain of command: defining responsibilities, such as who gives the order to evacuate and who contacts the external teams (115 and 122)."]},{heading:"2. Survival supplies and sustained resources (survival logistics)",body:"Preparing life-sustaining supplies for the first minutes of an incident and for the first 72 hours of a major disaster:",points:["Medical supplies: trauma kits containing equipment for controlling life-threatening bleeding (such as tourniquets and packing gauze).","Backup communications: VHF/UHF radios and satellite telephones for when communication networks are down.","Essential resources: preparing water and battery or generator power for the management teams while they remain in the disaster area."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_15_emergency_drills.mp4",title:"Field exercises",description:"How to conduct evacuation drills in government offices"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the main purpose of assembly points?",options:[{text:"To show off the beauty of the building",correct:!1},{text:"To account for employees and confirm that everyone is safe",correct:!0},{text:"To collect waste",correct:!1},{text:"To park vehicles",correct:!1}],explanation:"Assembly points are used to establish whether everyone has left the building or whether someone remains in danger."}]},{id:16,code:"SAR-1600",icon:"Construction",accent:"#16a34a",title:"Advanced search and rescue (USAR)",subtitle:"Urban Search and Rescue & Structural Collapse",summary:"Techniques for rescue from beneath rubble, the INSARAG marking system and the use of listening and search devices.",blocks:[{body:"Urban Search and Rescue (USAR) is sensitive and dangerous work. After earthquakes or the collapse of tall buildings, specialist teams need engineering knowledge in order to understand how concrete elements move. This module has been prepared in accordance with the international INSARAG guidelines."},{heading:"1. Phases of rescue",body:"The rescue process is carried out in the following order:",points:["General assessment (size-up / reconnaissance): assessing the extent of the damage, the stability of the structure and hazards such as gas and electricity.","Surface rescue: extricating people who are visible and easily reached.","Void space search: locating casualties in the voids and spaces of collapsed buildings using miniature cameras and listening devices.","Selected debris removal: carefully removing specific pieces with hand tools and hydraulic tools in order to reach casualties; general debris removal with heavy machinery follows."]},{heading:"2. The international marking system (INSARAG marking)",body:"USAR teams place standard markings on the walls of buildings in order to leave information for the teams that follow them:",points:["Worksite marking: under INSARAG, the team writes the worksite ID (sector letter and worksite number, e.g., C-6) on the building so that all teams know it by the same name. The four-quadrant X system (team name, time and date, hazards, victims) is the FEMA system used in the United States.","Victim marking: a large letter (V) is written near the location of a potential or confirmed victim; beneath it, (L) followed by a number for live victims and (D) followed by a number for the deceased. When a victim is removed, the marking is crossed out."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_16_search_tech.mp4",title:"Search technology",description:"Using listening devices and radar to detect the sounds and movements of casualties beneath rubble"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_16_marking_standard.png",title:"INSARAG guidelines",description:"How to record information on collapsed buildings"}],quiz:[{question:"Under INSARAG, what does the letter (L) beneath the (V) marking on a building wall indicate?",options:[{text:"A gas leak",correct:!1},{text:"The presence of live victims (Live)",correct:!0},{text:"Total collapse",correct:!1},{text:"The search has been completed",correct:!1}],explanation:"L stands for Live; together with a number, it shows how many confirmed live victims are at that location."}]},{id:17,code:"TRAFFIC-1700",icon:"CarFront",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Road traffic collisions and technical extrication",subtitle:"Advanced Vehicle Extrication & Road Safety",summary:"Managing serious road traffic collisions, using hydraulic rescue tools (Jaws of Life) and protecting the spine.",blocks:[{body:"Responding to road traffic collisions is among the main daily tasks of the Civil Defense. In this module we discuss the stages of extricating casualties from vehicles without causing further injury to the spine and neck. In every collision involving casualties, call 115 and 122."},{heading:"1. Scene safety",body:"Before any intervention, the scene must be made safe:",points:["Warning zone: establish the danger zone and place signs and cones ahead of the collision at a distance appropriate to the road speed, in coordination with the traffic police (104).","Vehicle stabilization: use wooden blocks or supports (cribbing/blocks) beneath the vehicle and in front of and behind the tires so that it does not move during the operation.","Energy isolation: disconnect the negative battery cable to reduce the risk of fire and of accidental airbag deployment. Note: airbags can remain live for a short time even after the battery has been disconnected, and hybrid and electric vehicles have high-voltage systems."]},{heading:'2. Extrication techniques with the "Jaws of Life"',body:"Using hydraulic power to reshape the vehicle body:",points:["Cutting: cutting the vehicle's pillars (A, B, C) so that the roof can be removed completely.","Spreading: forcing open buckled doors and creating space for the medical teams.","Neck protection: before any movement, stabilize the casualty's head with your hands (manual in-line stabilization), and if required by the medical protocol, apply a cervical collar (C-collar)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_17_extrication_tech.mp4",title:"Technical extrication",description:"How metal is cut with hydraulic tools"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_17_airbag_safety.png",title:"Airbag safety",description:"The dangers of accidental airbag deployment during rescue"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is vehicle stabilization the first step before extricating a casualty?",options:[{text:"For appearance",correct:!1},{text:"So that the vehicle does not move and the casualty's neck and back injuries are not made worse",correct:!0},{text:"To clear the road",correct:!1},{text:"It is not important",correct:!1}],explanation:"Any movement of the vehicle while metal is being cut can damage the spine and cause permanent paralysis of the casualty."}]},{id:18,code:"IND-1800",icon:"Factory",accent:"#4b5563",title:"Industrial and factory safety",subtitle:"Industrial Safety & Factory Risk Management",summary:"Preventing industrial accidents, occupational safety standards (OSHA) and the LOTO system for energy isolation.",blocks:[{body:"Factories and industrial zones are full of complex hazards. Under occupational safety standards (such as OSHA in the United States and the international standard ISO 45001), every institution must have a risk assessment system. This module teaches you how to provide a safe working environment based on engineering principles."},{heading:"1. The LOTO system (lockout/tagout)",body:"The safety standard for machine maintenance (OSHA 1910.147):",points:["Lockout: physically locking the main electrical switch or the pressure valve.","Tagout: attaching a warning tag bearing the name of the person carrying out the work, so that no one switches the machine on.","Zero energy state: verifying that no energy (electrical, pressure, thermal or mechanical) remains in the system before work begins."]},{heading:"2. Machine guarding and PPE",body:"Preventing body contact with moving parts:",points:["Machine guarding: installing metal barriers around the belts and sharp wheels of machines.","Protective equipment: using steel-toe footwear, acid-resistant gloves and hearing protection in places where noise exceeds an average of 85 decibels (dBA) over 8 hours."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/gov_18_loto_training.mp4",title:"The LOTO protocol",description:"How energy lockout is implemented in factories"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/gov_18_ppe_factory.png",title:"PPE",description:"A checklist of protective equipment for industrial workers"}],quiz:[{question:'What is meant by "zero energy state" in machine maintenance?',options:[{text:"Using a low level of electricity",correct:!1},{text:"Verifying that no residual energy remains in the machine, so that it cannot discharge or move",correct:!0},{text:"Not operating the machine at night",correct:!1},{text:"None of these",correct:!1}],explanation:"All stored pressure and electricity (in batteries and capacitors) must be released so that the machine does not start up unexpectedly during maintenance."}]},{id:19,code:"HIGH-1900",icon:"Building",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Firefighting in high-rise buildings",subtitle:"High-Rise Fire Dynamics & Standpipe Operations",summary:"The physical characteristics of fire in high-rise buildings, standpipe systems, the stack effect and evacuation strategies.",blocks:[{body:"High-rise buildings are a major challenge for firefighting teams, because reaching the upper floors with vehicle-mounted aerial ladders is often impossible. In this module we discuss air dynamics in high-rise buildings and how the building's internal water systems are used to extinguish fires at heights of more than 23 meters (75 feet)."},{heading:"1. The stack effect and smoke movement",body:"In high-rise buildings, the temperature difference between the inside and outside of the building creates an air movement known as the stack effect:",points:["Stack effect: in winter, warm air inside the building rises and rapidly spreads smoke through stairwells and elevator shafts.","Reverse stack effect: in summer, cool air-conditioned air flows downward through vertical shafts and can carry smoke down to the lower floors.","Mushrooming: when smoke reaches the top of a building or an enclosed space, it spreads horizontally and then banks back down, filling the upper floors."]},{heading:"2. Standpipe and FDC system engineering",body:"Because fire engines cannot easily deliver water to very high floors, firefighters rely on a fixed piping system (NFPA 14):",points:["Class I system: 2.5-inch hose outlets used only by firefighting teams, with their own hoses.","Fire department connection (FDC): the inlet on the outside of the building through which a fire engine pumps water to boost the system pressure for the upper floors.","Pressure-regulating valves: valves that regulate pressure so that excessive water pressure on the lower floors does not endanger firefighters and hoses."]},{heading:"3. Evacuation protocols and elevator recall",body:"Managing occupants in high-rise buildings requires detailed plans to protect life:",points:["Do not use the elevators: during a fire, elevators enter Phase I recall, travel to the main floor and stop there so that people are not trapped inside; thereafter only firefighters may use them in Phase II mode.","Phased evacuation: first evacuate the occupants of the fire floor and the floors immediately above and below it, then the other floors in sequence.","Areas of refuge: protected areas on the floors for people with disabilities who cannot descend by the stairs."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_19_highrise_tactics.mp4",title:"High-rise tactics",description:"How fires are extinguished on very high floors"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_19_standpipe_diagram.png",title:"Standpipe diagram",description:"An analysis of the water piping system inside the building"}],quiz:[{question:"What causes the stack effect in high-rise buildings?",options:[{text:"An electrical fault",correct:!1},{text:"The temperature difference between the inside and outside of the building",correct:!0},{text:"A shortage of water",correct:!1},{text:"Only the height of the building",correct:!1}],explanation:"The stack effect is caused by differences in air temperature and density, and it moves air upward or downward through vertical shafts (stairwells and elevator shafts)."}]},{id:20,code:"WILD-2000",icon:"TreePine",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Wildland and vegetation firefighting",subtitle:"Wildfire Dynamics & Vegetation Fire Safety",summary:"Wildfire dynamics, the influence of weather, the design of defensible space and firebreak techniques for stopping fire spread.",blocks:[{body:"Forest and grassland fires (wildfires) are a distinct hazard because the weather and the wind direction directly affect them. In the Kurdistan Region, summer fires damage wheat fields and forests every year. In this module we discuss scientific strategies for preventing and extinguishing these fires."},{heading:"1. The wildfire behavior triangle",body:"The behavior of an outdoor fire depends on three main factors:",points:["Fuel: the type of trees and dry vegetation and their moisture content, which determine how intensely the fire spreads.","Topography: fire travels much faster uphill on slopes, because heat and flames rise and preheat the fuel above.","Weather: temperature, strong wind and low humidity are the three main factors in the spread of fire."]},{heading:"2. Types of wildfires",body:"Fires are classified according to the level at which burning occurs:",points:["Surface fire: the burning of grass and leaves on the ground, which is easier to control.","Crown fire: the most dangerous type; the fire reaches the treetops and, driven by the wind, spreads at very high speed.","Spotting: embers and burning brands are carried by the wind and start new fires farther away (sometimes several kilometers)."]},{heading:"3. Protection strategy: defensible space",body:"To protect villages and buildings near forests, three zones are defined according to CAL FIRE guidelines:",points:["Zone 0 (0–1.5 meters): a zone with no fuel; there must be no trees, dry vegetation or combustible items next to the wall.","Zone 1 (1.5–9 meters): reducing tree density, regularly clearing dry leaves and spacing trees apart.","Zone 2 (9–30 meters): reducing fuel and removing the lower branches of trees, so that fire cannot climb from the ground to the treetops (ladder fuels)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_20_wildfire_behavior.mp4",title:"Fire behavior in mountains",description:"How fire spreads according to wind direction and slope"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_20_defensible_space.png",title:"Protection map",description:"Defining safety zones around houses"}],quiz:[{question:"Why does fire spread faster on mountain slopes than on flat ground?",options:[{text:"Because there are more trees there",correct:!1},{text:"Because heat and flames rise and preheat the fuel above",correct:!0},{text:"Because the wind on the mountaintop is cold",correct:!1},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to the laws of physics, heat rises; therefore, the steeper the slope, the faster the fire spreads uphill."}]},{id:21,code:"WATER-2100",icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Water rescue and flood hydrology",subtitle:"Advanced Water Rescue & Drowning Prevention",summary:"An analysis of the hydraulic force of water, emergency rescue techniques and drowning prevention.",blocks:[{body:"Drowning is one of the leading causes of death during outings and disasters. Understanding the force of water and how the body functions underwater (the mammalian dive reflex) is very important for rescue teams. In this module we discuss the science of water rescue and protection protocols. In any water incident, call 115 and 122."},{heading:"1. The physics of fast-moving water (swiftwater hydrology)",body:"Fast-moving water generates an enormous force that a person cannot withstand:",points:["Force of water: if the speed of the water doubles, its force on a body or a vehicle increases fourfold.","Strainers: obstacles in the water such as tree branches or nets that let water pass through but trap solid objects (such as a person), causing rapid drowning.","Eddies: calm areas behind large rocks that rescue teams use for resting and approaching."]},{heading:"2. The golden rules of water rescue",body:"To protect the rescuer's life, this sequence must be followed:",points:["REACH: use a pole or a ladder without entering the water yourself.","THROW: throw a rope or something that floats (such as a tire).","ROW: use a boat to approach the casualty.","GO: the last option, only for trained specialists wearing personal flotation devices (PFDs).","HELO: the use of helicopters by specialized teams in major floods."]},{heading:"3. First aid for drowning victims",body:"When a person has been brought out of the water, every second counts:",points:["Breathing: drowning is fundamentally a problem of oxygen deprivation; if the person is unconscious and not breathing normally, start CPR and add rescue breaths if you are trained (according to the ERC, beginning with 5 initial breaths).","Hypothermia: people brought out of the water often suffer from hypothermia; remove wet clothing and rewarm them gradually.",'Delayed lung deterioration: anyone who has been brought out of the water and has a cough, shortness of breath or confusion must be examined by a doctor, because lung deterioration can appear after several hours. Outdated terms such as "secondary/dry drowning" are no longer used medically.']}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_21_swiftwater_rescue.mp4",title:"Emergency rescue",description:"International techniques for extracting people from fast-moving water"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_21_hydrology.png",title:"Analysis of the force of water",description:"Demonstrating the force of water on the human body"}],quiz:[{question:"What is the first safe step in rescuing a person in the water?",options:[{text:"Jumping into the water",correct:!1},{text:"REACH (extending a pole or an object)",correct:!0},{text:"ROW (using a boat)",correct:!1},{text:"Waiting until the teams arrive",correct:!1}],explanation:"REACH is the safest method, because you do not enter the water and your own life is not put at risk."},{question:"Why should people rescued from the water who have signs such as coughing or shortness of breath go to the hospital, even if they appear well?",options:[{text:"To check their clothing",correct:!1},{text:"Because lung deterioration can appear after several hours",correct:!0},{text:"To change their name",correct:!1},{text:"There is no need",correct:!1}],explanation:"After drowning, water that has entered the lungs can gradually cause lung deterioration, and symptoms may appear after several hours; a medical examination is therefore necessary."}]},{id:22,code:"WEATHER-2200",icon:"CloudLightning",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Severe weather and natural emergencies",subtitle:"Severe Weather Emergencies & Survival",summary:"An analysis of lightning, high winds and dust storms, and of the measures for protecting the body against extreme cold and heat.",blocks:[{body:"Severe weather can cause major disasters and loss of life within a short time. In the Kurdistan Region we face lightning, severe dust storms and very high or very low temperatures. Recognizing the warning signs and knowing the protective measures before an event is the key to survival."},{heading:"1. The lightning phenomenon (lightning safety)",body:"Lightning can carry up to approximately 300 million volts and tens of thousands of amperes (NOAA), so protection is essential:",points:["Shelter: the best places during lightning are enclosed buildings or fully enclosed vehicles with a metal roof.","Keep away: never stand under tall trees, and keep away from open and elevated areas and from water.","If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning: go indoors immediately (When Thunder Roars, Go Indoors), and stay there for 30 minutes after the last thunder. The older 30-30 rule used the same 30-minute waiting period.","Electrical appliances: at home, unplug sensitive appliances so that a sudden voltage spike (power surge) does not damage them."]},{heading:"2. Dust storms and high winds",body:"Dust storms are a major health and traffic hazard in our region:",points:["Driving: if visibility becomes very poor, pull completely off the road and stop on the right-hand side, switch off the vehicle's lights and apply the parking brake, so that other vehicles do not follow your lights and collide with you.","Respiratory protection: use N95 masks or a damp cloth to reduce the entry of fine particles into the lungs.","High winds: during high winds, keep away from glass windows and stay in interior rooms of the building."]},{heading:"3. Temperature emergencies (heat and cold stress)",body:"Every first aid provider needs to understand hypothermia and heat stroke:",points:["Heat stroke: when the body temperature exceeds 40 °C and changes in mental status (confusion, disorientation) appear. The skin may be dry, red and hot, but in exertional heat stroke from work or exercise there may be sweating. This is a life-threatening condition: call 122 and cool the body with cold water immediately.","Hypothermia: when the body's core temperature falls below 35 °C. Signs: violent shivering, slurred speech and a slowing heart rate.","Frostbite: the freezing of body tissue (fingers, ears, nose) as a result of very severe cold; never rub the frostbitten area and never rewarm it with very hot water."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_22_weather_survival.mp4",title:"Protective measures",description:"How to deal with lightning and high winds"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_22_heat_stroke.png",title:"Signs of heat illness",description:"The difference between heat exhaustion and life-threatening heat stroke"}],quiz:[{question:"What does the 30-30 rule refer to during lightning?",options:[{text:"The temperature",correct:!1},{text:"Determining the danger distance and the waiting time after lightning",correct:!0},{text:"The wind speed",correct:!1},{text:"The amount of rainfall",correct:!1}],explanation:"The older rule says: if the time between the lightning flash and its thunder is less than 30 seconds, you must go to an enclosed place and stay there for 30 minutes after the last thunder. The newer NOAA guidance is simpler: if you hear thunder, go indoors immediately."},{question:"Which of the following is a sign of heat stroke?",options:[{text:"Heavy sweating only",correct:!1},{text:"A rise in body temperature to above 40 °C with confusion, and dry, red skin",correct:!0},{text:"Shivering",correct:!1},{text:"Sneezing",correct:!1}],explanation:"In heat stroke, the body loses its ability to cool itself and its temperature rises above 40 °C, which damages the brain. Immediate cooling and a call to 122 are required."}]},{id:23,code:"SUPP-2300",icon:"BellRing",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire alarm and fire suppression systems",subtitle:"Fire Detection & Automatic Suppression Engineering",summary:"A technical analysis of fire detectors, sprinkler systems and clean agent extinguishing technology.",blocks:[{body:"Automatic protection systems are the first line of defense in modern buildings. These systems are designed to detect a fire in its incipient stage and to control it without human intervention. This module explains the engineering principles in accordance with the standards NFPA 13 (sprinklers) and NFPA 72 (fire alarms)."},{heading:"1. Detection and alarm systems",body:"Detectors are the eyes of the building and operate by several methods:",points:["Photoelectric smoke detector: relies on a beam of light; it is the best type for detecting slow, smoldering fires.","Ionization detector: more sensitive to the very small particles produced by fast, flaming fires.","Heat detectors: used in places where smoke or steam is normally present (such as kitchens and factories), and activated by a rise in temperature.","Notification appliances: the use of alarm sounders and flashing lights to alert the entire building."]},{heading:"2. Sprinkler system engineering",body:"This system uses water pressure to control a fire at its point of origin:",points:["Glass bulb: most sprinkler heads have a liquid-filled glass bulb that breaks at a specified temperature and allows water to discharge.","System types: wet pipe, which is the most common and in which the pipes are filled with water, and dry pipe for places where there is a risk of the water freezing.","A scientific fact: only the heads exposed to the heat of the fire open, not all the heads in the building at once (as shown in movies)."]},{heading:"3. Extinguishing with clean agents (clean agent systems)",body:"In places where water would damage the contents (such as server rooms and museums), gaseous agents are used (NFPA 2001):",points:["FM-200 and FK-5-1-12 (Novec 1230): these agents act mainly by absorbing heat and also partly break the chain reaction, and they leave no residue.","CO₂ systems: very effective but pose an asphyxiation hazard to people, so they are generally used only in unoccupied spaces, with a pre-discharge alarm and system lockout (NFPA 12).","Saponification: the method of extinguishing kitchen fires (Class K under NFPA, Class F under EN 2) by converting the burning oil into a soap-like substance."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_23_sprinkler_physics.mp4",title:"Sprinkler physics",description:"An analysis of how the automatic water system operates"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_23_detector_types.png",title:"Types of detectors",description:"A comparison of how smoke and heat detectors work"}],quiz:[{question:"Which type of smoke detector is better at detecting slow, smoldering fires?",options:[{text:"Ionization detector",correct:!1},{text:"Photoelectric detector",correct:!0},{text:"Heat detector",correct:!1},{text:"Flame detector",correct:!1}],explanation:"Photoelectric detectors use a beam of light to detect thick smoke very quickly, which is characteristic of slow fires."},{question:"In a standard sprinkler system, do all the sprinkler heads open at the same time?",options:[{text:"Yes, they all operate together",correct:!1},{text:"No, only the head in which the heat reaches the bulb's breaking temperature opens",correct:!0},{text:"Only if the firefighting teams give the order",correct:!1},{text:"Only at night",correct:!1}],explanation:"A standard system operates locally; each head operates independently so that water damage in other areas is reduced."}]},{id:24,code:"EVAC-2400",icon:"Tent",accent:"#0d9488",title:"Mass evacuation and shelter management",subtitle:"Mass Evacuation Strategy & Shelter Operations",summary:"Mass evacuation strategies in major disasters, the organization of relief camps and compliance with the international humanitarian standards for shelters (Sphere).",blocks:[{body:"In major disasters such as devastating earthquakes or widespread floods, evacuating thousands of people to safe locations requires a complex logistical plan. Shelter management is not only about providing space; it is about ensuring health, security and human dignity in accordance with international standards (such as the Sphere Handbook). This module explains the principles of organizing people and shelters."},{heading:"1. Strategic evacuation planning",body:"The process of evacuating people must follow organized phases so that confusion does not arise:",points:["Designating evacuation routes: mapping the main and alternative routes, and designating the routes reserved for emergency vehicles only.","Traffic management: coordinating with the traffic police (104) to prevent congestion and the blocking of exit routes.","Public warning system: using loudspeakers, SMS, radio and local television to inform people when the evacuation begins."]},{heading:"2. Managing shelters and emergency camps",body:"According to international standards (Sphere), every shelter must meet the following conditions:",points:["Site classification: distinguishing between short-term shelters (such as schools and sports halls) and long-term camps (such as tented camps).","Registration and database: recording the names of all displaced persons, and separately registering families and individuals who are alone (particularly unaccompanied children) in order to protect their security.","Health and hygiene: providing at least 15 liters of water per person per day (for drinking, cooking and personal hygiene), and one toilet for every 20 people (at the onset of an emergency, one for every 50 people as the minimum)."]},{heading:"3. Care of vulnerable groups",body:"During disasters, certain groups need special protection:",points:["Older people and people with disabilities: providing suitable routes for wheelchairs and providing medicines for chronic illnesses.","Children: setting up dedicated, protected areas (child-friendly spaces) so that they are kept away from psychological harm and other dangers.","Pregnant women: providing dedicated medical teams in the shelter for unexpected births."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_24_shelter_org.mp4",title:"Camp organization",description:"How tents and services are laid out according to international standards"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_24_evac_map.png",title:"Evacuation map",description:"An example of a city evacuation plan during a flood"}],quiz:[{question:"According to the humanitarian standards (Sphere), how many liters of water per day does each person in a shelter need as a minimum?",options:[{text:"2 liters",correct:!1},{text:"5 liters",correct:!1},{text:"15 liters",correct:!0},{text:"50 liters",correct:!1}],explanation:"The Sphere standard states that at least 15 liters are needed for drinking, hygiene and cooking."},{question:"What is the main purpose of child-friendly spaces in camps?",options:[{text:"For children to sleep",correct:!1},{text:"To protect children from psychological harm and provide a protected play area",correct:!0},{text:"To store food",correct:!1},{text:"For learning languages",correct:!1}],explanation:"These spaces are set up so that children are in a calm environment and are not exposed to violence or psychological problems after the disaster."}]},{id:25,code:"TECH-2500",icon:"Mountain",accent:"#475569",title:"Technical and field rescue",subtitle:"Technical Rescue: Rope, Confined Space & Structural Collapse",summary:"Rope rescue, rescue from confined spaces and heights, and the engineering of stabilizing collapsed structures.",blocks:[{body:"Technical rescue is an advanced level of civil defense work in which specialized teams rescue casualties using engineering equipment and mechanical systems. This work requires detailed planning and knowledge of physics so that it does not cause further harm to the casualty or to the team."},{heading:"1. Rope rescue",body:"The use of rope systems in accordance with the NFPA 2500 standard (which has replaced the former NFPA 1670, NFPA 1858 and NFPA 1983):",points:["Two systems: use of a main line and a belay line; there must always be two systems so that if one fails, the other protects the casualty and the rescuer.","Mechanical advantage: using 3:1 or 5:1 systems with pulleys to raise a casualty or heavy loads with the least possible muscular effort.","Essential knots: using knots such as the figure 8 on a bight and the double fisherman's, which do not slip under heavy load."]},{heading:"2. Confined space rescue",body:"Spaces such as fuel tanks, sewer pipes and underground pits are silent hazards (OSHA 1910.146):",points:["Atmospheric testing: before entry, the oxygen level (at least 19.5%), flammable gases (below 10% of the LEL) and toxic gases such as H₂S and CO must be tested.","Team roles: the team is divided into three roles: the entrant (who goes inside), the attendant (who monitors from outside and never goes in) and the supervisor (the commander).","Forced ventilation: using air blowers to remove toxic gases and bring in clean air before the team enters and throughout the operation."]},{heading:"3. Structural collapse rescue",body:"Stabilizing collapsed buildings so that teams can safely enter the voids:",points:["Shoring: using timber and steel props to support ceilings and walls that are at risk of collapse.","Search equipment: using acoustic and seismic listening devices to hear the tapping and voices of casualties, and radar devices to detect their breathing and movements beneath rubble.","K9 teams: using trained dogs to detect the scent of living people in large rubble piles."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_25_rope_systems.mp4",title:"Rope systems",description:"An analysis of the 3:1 system for raising casualties in mountainous terrain"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_25_confined_entry.mp4",title:"Confined space entry",description:"Safety procedures and the use of atmospheric monitoring equipment"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_25_shoring_types.png",title:"Types of shoring",description:"Demonstrating engineering methods for supporting walls at risk of collapse"}],quiz:[{question:"Why are two systems (main and belay) always required in rope rescue?",options:[{text:"For greater speed",correct:!1},{text:"So that if one rope or system fails, the other protects the lives of the casualty and the team",correct:!0},{text:"So that there is more equipment",correct:!1},{text:"Only for appearance",correct:!1}],explanation:"According to international standards, the belay (backup) system is a fundamental safety requirement when working at height."},{question:"What is the minimum oxygen concentration at which entry into a confined space without breathing apparatus is permitted?",options:[{text:"10%",correct:!1},{text:"16%",correct:!1},{text:"19.5%",correct:!0},{text:"21%",correct:!1}],explanation:"If oxygen is below 19.5%, the atmosphere is oxygen-deficient and the team must not enter without a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA)."}]},{id:26,code:"COMM-2550",icon:"Radio",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Emergency communications and dispatch systems",subtitle:"Emergency Communications & Radio Dispatch Systems",summary:"Radio communication systems, the call-taking and dispatch protocol, interagency interoperability and the use of plain language.",blocks:[{body:"Effective communication is the backbone of all disaster management. In this module we discuss digital and analog radio systems, the dispatch protocol and how coordination is achieved between different agencies (police, firefighting, ambulance). Understanding these systems is essential for every civil defense officer."},{heading:"1. Radio systems",body:"Types of wireless communication systems:",points:["VHF/UHF: common frequencies for local communication; in general, VHF is better for open areas and UHF is better inside buildings.","Trunked radio: a system (today usually digital) that automatically shares multiple frequencies among different groups, such as the P25 systems in the United States.","Satellite communications: for remote areas or when terrestrial networks are down.","LTE/FirstNet: a dedicated network for emergency teams in the United States that gives communications priority and high quality."]},{heading:"2. The call-taking and dispatch protocol",body:"Standard procedures for receiving emergency calls and dispatching teams:",points:["Call-taking: using a CAD (computer-aided dispatch) system to record information quickly.","Priority classification: determining the level of risk (Priority 1, 2, 3) according to the type of incident and the content of the call.","EMD protocol (emergency medical dispatch): giving instructions to the caller by telephone (for example, CPR instructions) until the ambulance arrives.","Response time: under NFPA 1225 (formerly NFPA 1221), 90% of emergency calls must be answered within 15 seconds and 95% within 20 seconds; team arrival times are measured against the Directorate's internal targets."]},{heading:"3. Plain language and standard radio terminology",body:"Under NIMS/ICS, radio communications are conducted in simple, clear language, not in codes, so that all agencies understand one another:",points:['Ten-codes: codes such as 10-4 and 10-20 are a legacy system, and their meaning differs from one agency to another; they are therefore not used at joint incidents. Instead of "10-4" say "Understood," and instead of "10-20" state your location by name and landmark.',"Phonetic alphabet: using Alfa, Bravo, Charlie... to clarify letters over the radio.","Mayday: the international distress call (spoken three times) used when a firefighter is in life-threatening danger; everyone must clear the channel for them.","PAR (personnel accountability report): a check that all team members at the scene are accounted for."]},{heading:"4. Interagency interoperability",body:"How communication is achieved between different agencies:",points:["Shared channels: designating specific frequencies for communication between the police, firefighting and ambulance services.","Gateway devices: devices that link different systems together (such as VHF with UHF).","Communications in ICS: the Communications Unit Leader is responsible for preparing the communications plan.","NIMS typing: classifying communications resources according to national standards (Type I, II, III)."]},{heading:"5. Command centers",body:"Establishing central locations for controlling communications:",points:["EOC (emergency operations center): the main center for disaster management at the city or governorate level.","Mobile command: a special vehicle that serves as a field command center.","Status boards: display boards showing the location of teams and the status of the incident.","Redundancy: having backup systems in case the main system fails."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_26_radio_ops.mp4",title:"Working with the radio",description:"The principles of radio communication and the phonetic alphabet"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_26_dispatch_center.png",title:"The emergency call center",description:"Organization of the CAD room and its equipment"}],quiz:[{question:'What does the word "Mayday" mean on the radio?',options:[{text:"The start of an operation",correct:!1},{text:"A firefighter's distress call in a life-threatening situation",correct:!0},{text:"The end of an operation",correct:!1},{text:"A radio check",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mayday is the international distress call used when a firefighter's life is in serious danger and immediate assistance is needed."},{question:"What is the main difference between VHF and UHF?",options:[{text:"VHF is better inside buildings",correct:!1},{text:"UHF is better for open areas",correct:!1},{text:"VHF is better for open areas and UHF is better inside buildings",correct:!0},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1}],explanation:"VHF waves are longer and carry better over open areas, whereas UHF penetrates buildings more easily."}]},{id:27,code:"CBRN-2600",icon:"Radiation",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Defense against lethal agents (CBRN)",subtitle:"Chemical, Biological, Radiological & Nuclear Defense",summary:"Protection strategies against chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear threats, PPE levels and decontamination procedures.",blocks:[{body:"The term CBRN refers to four types of lethal hazards: chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear. This module has been prepared for officers and engineers so that they know how to respond to non-routine situations. Here we discuss the physics of radiation, the chemistry of nerve agents and mass decontamination protocols in accordance with international standards."},{heading:"1. Chemical threats (chemical agents)",body:"Chemical agents are classified according to their effects on the body:",points:["Nerve agents: such as sarin and VX; they disable the nervous system. Their signs are recognized by the mnemonic SLUDGEM (pinpoint pupils, tearing, urination, diarrhea and muscle twitching).","Choking agents: such as chlorine and phosgene; they cause severe damage to the lungs.","Blister agents: such as mustard gas; they cause severe chemical burns to the skin, eyes and lungs.","Blood agents: such as cyanide; they prevent cells from using oxygen."]},{heading:"2. Biological and microbial threats",body:"The use of bacteria, viruses and biological toxins as weapons:",points:["Bacteria: such as anthrax and plague; anthrax spores can persist in the environment for a very long time.","Viruses: such as Ebola and the smallpox virus, which can spread from person to person.","Biological toxins: such as ricin, which is produced from the seeds of the castor plant and is an extremely lethal poison.","Measures: using HEPA filters and negative-pressure rooms to isolate infected people."]},{heading:"3. Radiation physics and nuclear protection",body:"Understanding the three principles of protection (time, distance and shielding):",points:["Types of radiation: alpha is stopped by a sheet of paper, beta requires plastic or aluminum, but gamma is attenuated only by dense, thick materials such as lead, concrete or earth.","Dirty bomb: a conventional explosive containing radioactive material in order to contaminate an area; it is not a nuclear explosion.","The TDS principle: Time (minimize the time spent near the source), Distance (increase the distance from it) and Shielding (place a barrier between yourself and the source)."]},{heading:"4. Levels of protective clothing (PPE levels)",body:"Civil Defense personnel must know which suit to use and when:",points:["Level A: the highest protection; a fully encapsulating, gas-tight suit with a self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA) worn inside the suit.","Level B: the highest respiratory protection (SCBA) with a liquid-splash-resistant suit, but the suit is not gas-tight.","Level C: an air-purifying respirator and a chemical suit; only when the substance and its concentration are known and oxygen is sufficient.","Level D: an ordinary work uniform with gloves; no chemical respiratory hazard."]},{heading:"5. The decontamination protocol",body:"The process of removing contaminants from the body and from equipment:",points:["Stages: removal of clothing (which, according to international guidance, removes approximately 80–90% of the contamination), washing with water and soap, and checking (with a Geiger counter for radioactive contamination).","Zones: the hot zone, which is contaminated; the warm zone, where decontamination takes place; and the cold zone, where the command post and medical teams are located.","Wind direction: the clean zone and the command post must always be upwind and on higher ground."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_27_cbrn_ppe.mp4",title:"Levels of protection",description:"How to don a Level A suit correctly"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_27_radiation_chart.png",title:"Types of radiation",description:"An analysis of the penetrating power of gamma and beta radiation"}],quiz:[{question:"Which PPE level provides full protection against gases and chemical contact?",options:[{text:"Level D",correct:!1},{text:"Level C",correct:!1},{text:"Level B",correct:!1},{text:"Level A",correct:!0}],explanation:"Level A is a fully encapsulating suit that protects the wearer from lethal chemical gases and liquids."},{question:"According to the TDS principle, how can you reduce the radiation hazard to your body?",options:[{text:"By reducing the time spent near the source and increasing the distance from it",correct:!0},{text:"By drinking water",correct:!1},{text:"By running in any direction",correct:!1},{text:"By using a simple mask",correct:!1}],explanation:"Time, distance and shielding are the three scientific principles for protecting people from radiation."}]},{id:28,code:"INVEST-2700",icon:"Search",accent:"#d97706",title:"Fire cause investigation",subtitle:"Scientific Fire & Arson Investigation",summary:"The science of determining the origin of a fire, the analysis of burn patterns and the NFPA 921 methodology.",blocks:[{body:"Fire cause investigation is a science that combines police work and engineering. The aim is to establish the facts of the incident: was the fire accidental, or was it set deliberately (arson)? This module is based on the NFPA 921 guide, which sets out the scientific method for collecting and analyzing evidence."},{heading:"1. Origin determination",body:"Finding the point at which the fire started:",points:["V-pattern: a mark in the shape of the letter V forms on walls and shows the direction of the fire from bottom to top. The base of this pattern is often close to the origin of the fire.","Lowest point of burning: because heat rises, the lowest location showing fire damage is often close to the point of origin (although after flashover these patterns can be altered).","Calcination: chemical changes in gypsum (drywall) walls that indicate how intensely and for how long the fire burned at that location."]},{heading:"2. Accelerants",body:"Identifying deliberate fires through fuel residues:",points:["Hydrocarbons: detecting residues of gasoline, diesel or kerosene in the debris.","Detection dogs: investigation teams use trained dogs (K9) that can detect the scent of accelerants within a short time; however, under NFPA 921, every canine alert must be confirmed by laboratory analysis.","Laboratory analysis: using gas chromatography–mass spectrometry (GC-MS) to confirm the presence of chemicals in samples of debris and soil."]},{heading:"3. Arson indicators",body:"Certain signs raise suspicion of a crime:",points:["Multiple origins: if the fire started in several places at the same time, with no connection between them.","Disabled systems: if the sprinkler or fire alarm system had been shut off in advance.","Trailers: the presence of a line of fuel or fabric on the floor to carry the fire from one room to another."]},{heading:"4. Evidence preservation (chain of custody)",body:"Legal procedures to ensure that evidence is accepted in court:",points:["Chain of custody: recording every person who has handled an item of evidence, from the time it is found until it reaches the laboratory and the court.","Documentation: taking close-up (macro) photographs of fine details and drawing a sketch of the scene.","Spoliation: guarding against the destruction of evidence during fire suppression; firefighters must be aware of burn patterns and must not disturb the scene after extinguishment."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_fire_patterns.mp4",title:"Burn patterns",description:"An analysis of the difference between a V-pattern and a clean burn"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_28_arson_dog.jpg",title:"K9 dogs",description:"The role of trained dogs in detecting accelerants"}],quiz:[{question:"What does a V-pattern on a building wall indicate?",options:[{text:"The direction in which people escaped",correct:!1},{text:"The direction in which the fire spread upward from its origin",correct:!0},{text:"An electrical fault",correct:!1},{text:"The rate at which the fire cooled",correct:!1}],explanation:"This pattern shows where the fire started and in which direction it traveled, helping investigators to find the point of origin."},{question:"Why is the chain of custody important in an investigation?",options:[{text:"To make the report look better",correct:!1},{text:"So that the evidence is accepted in court and cannot have been altered",correct:!0},{text:"To speed up firefighting",correct:!1},{text:"It has no importance",correct:!1}],explanation:"If the chain of custody is broken, defense lawyers can argue in court that the evidence was altered, and the case may be dismissed."}]},{id:29,code:"COMM-2800",icon:"RadioTower",accent:"#2563eb",title:"Communication systems and radio protocol",subtitle:"Emergency Communications & Radio Protocols",summary:"Radio voice procedures, emergency channels, plain language and keeping communications working during disasters.",blocks:[{body:"Communication is one of the most important elements during disasters. Without good communication there can be no coordination and teams cannot work together. This module shows how radios and communication systems are used in emergencies."},{heading:"1. Principles of radio communication",body:"The basic rules for speaking on the radio:",points:["Listen first, then speak: before transmitting, make sure the channel is clear.",'Response words: "Received," "Understood," "Affirmative."',"Phonetic alphabet: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie...","Brief and clear: keep your message short and clear.","Repetition: repeat important messages (locations, numbers, orders) or ask the receiver to read them back."]},{heading:"2. Plain language instead of codes",body:"Under NIMS/ICS, at incidents involving several agencies, communication is conducted in simple, clear language. Ten-codes are a legacy, local system, and their meaning differs from one agency to another. Examples:",points:['Instead of the old code "10-4," say: "Received" or "Understood."',`Instead of "10-33," say: "Emergency, immediate assistance required" (when a firefighter's life is in danger: Mayday).`,'Instead of "10-9," say: "Say again."','Instead of "10-20," state your location by name and landmark, for example: "I am on the third floor, north side."',"Over: my message is finished and I am waiting for a reply / Out: the conversation is finished."]},{heading:"3. Communication systems",body:"Types of communication systems:",points:["VHF/UHF: standard handheld and vehicle radios.","Satellite: for when terrestrial networks are not working.","Push-to-talk (PTT): talking by pressing a button, via a radio or mobile applications.","Repeater: a device that amplifies and retransmits the signal over a longer range.","Mesh network: a self-organizing network in which each device relays messages to other devices."]},{heading:"4. Coordination during disasters",body:"How teams are coordinated:",points:["Coordination channel: a designated shared channel for all teams at the incident, in accordance with the communications plan.","Command channel: only unit leaders and the incident commander speak on this channel.","Scheduled reports: such as the PAR (personnel accountability report), according to the incident plan or at times of significant change.","Distress call: Mayday is spoken three times (Mayday, Mayday, Mayday), and everyone clears the channel.","GPS: tracking the location of each team."]},{heading:"5. Backup methods when networks fail",body:"When normal communications are not working:",points:["Runners: people who deliver messages by hand or in writing.","Prearranged assembly point: all teams return to an agreed location at a set time.","GPS tracker: a location-tracking device for each team.","Light signals: with a lamp or flashlight, particularly at night.","Sound signals: designated whistle or bell signals whose meaning all teams know."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_radio.mp4",title:"Using the radio",description:"How to speak on the radio"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_28_phonetic.png",title:"The phonetic alphabet",description:"The phonetic alphabet with examples"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_28_coordination.mp4",title:"Coordination",description:"Coordinating teams"}],quiz:[{question:'How is the old code "10-4" expressed in NIMS plain language?',options:[{text:"Emergency",correct:!1},{text:"Received / Understood",correct:!0},{text:"My location",correct:!1},{text:"Say again",correct:!1}],explanation:'10-4 is an old code meaning "received"; under NIMS/ICS, instead of the code, plain language is used: "Received" or "Understood," because codes do not have the same meaning in every agency.'},{question:'When is the word "Mayday" used?',options:[{text:"As a greeting",correct:!1},{text:"In a serious emergency: someone's life is in danger and help is needed",correct:!0},{text:"At the end of an operation",correct:!1},{text:"During a rest break",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mayday is the word for a serious emergency: it means that someone's life (particularly a firefighter's) is in danger and immediate help is needed."}]},{id:30,code:"LOG-2900",icon:"Package",accent:"#059669",title:"Logistics and resource management in disasters",subtitle:"Disaster Logistics & Supply Chain Management",summary:"Supplying food, medicine and materials to thousands of people, and managing warehouses and transportation.",blocks:[{body:"Disaster logistics is the science of delivering supplies and services to people in need, within a short time and in a fair manner. This module is important for leaders and managers who need to know how to manage resources."},{heading:"1. Logistics planning",body:"Advance preparation:",points:["Warehouses: designating storage locations for supplies before a disaster.","Supply list: food, water, medicine, tents and blankets.","Transportation: vehicles, aircraft and other means.","Teams: trained logistics personnel.","Relationships: contracts and relationships with suppliers."]},{heading:"2. Warehouse management",body:"How supplies are stored and distributed:",points:["Inventory: counting and recording all supplies.","Coding: a specific code for each type of supply.","Expiration: monitoring the expiration dates of food and medicines (whatever expires first is distributed first).","Protection: from heat, cold and humidity.","Security: preventing theft and damage."]},{heading:"3. Transportation and distribution",body:"Delivering supplies to where they are needed:",points:["Vehicles: within the city and to nearby areas.","Aircraft: to distant or isolated locations.","Helicopters: to mountainous locations and places where roads are blocked.","Distribution points: designated, clearly marked locations for distributing supplies.","Recording: who received what, and how much."]},{heading:"4. The supply chain",body:"Coordination with suppliers:",points:["Suppliers: supply companies and organizations.","Requests: how supplies are requested (requisition).","Delivery: specifying the time and place of delivery.","Finance: budget and payment.","Tracking: monitoring supplies from the supplier to the recipient."]},{heading:"5. Shelter management",body:"Shelter services (according to the Sphere standards):",points:["Tents: setting up and organizing the shelter.","Food: providing food for thousands of people.","Water: at least 15 liters of clean water per person per day.","Toilets and washing facilities: in sufficient numbers and in safe locations for women and children.","Health: medical services and first aid."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_29_logistics.mp4",title:"Disaster logistics",description:"The principles of logistics"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_29_warehouse.png",title:"Warehousing",description:"Warehouse management"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_29_distribution.mp4",title:"Distribution",description:"Distributing supplies"}],quiz:[{question:"What is disaster logistics?",options:[{text:"Extinguishing fires",correct:!1},{text:"Delivering supplies and services to people in need",correct:!0},{text:"First aid",correct:!1},{text:"Rescue",correct:!1}],explanation:"Disaster logistics is the science of delivering supplies and services (food, water, medicine) to people in need."},{question:"Why is it important to monitor the expiration dates of food and medicines?",options:[{text:"For the color",correct:!1},{text:"To protect people's health",correct:!0},{text:"It is pointless",correct:!1},{text:"For speed",correct:!1}],explanation:"Expired food and medicines can harm people's health, so monitoring expiration dates is very important."}]},{id:31,code:"TUNNEL-3000",icon:"Construction",accent:"#57534e",title:"Tunnel and underground space safety",subtitle:"Tunnel & Underground Space Safety",summary:"Fire dynamics in enclosed and underground spaces, rescue in long tunnels and the effects of smoke.",blocks:[{body:"Tunnels and underground spaces (such as road tunnels, mines and underground parking garages) present particular hazards. Fire in these spaces is far more dangerous because ventilation is limited, escape is difficult and smoke accumulates quickly. This module is important for road tunnels and underground parking garages in the cities of the Kurdistan Region (principal standard: NFPA 502)."},{heading:"1. Characteristics of tunnels",body:"How a tunnel differs from an ordinary building:",points:["Air: natural ventilation is limited and the fire consumes oxygen rapidly.","Exits: often there are only two portals, and in newer tunnels intermediate emergency doors (cross-passages).","Smoke: spreads quickly beneath the low ceiling and travels in two directions or in the direction of the ventilation.","Heat: the walls and ceiling radiate heat back, and heat builds up.","Length: it can be several kilometers, which lengthens the time it takes teams to reach the fire."]},{heading:"2. Fire dynamics in tunnels",body:"How fire behaves in a tunnel:",points:["Spread: in tunnels with longitudinal ventilation, smoke and heat travel in the direction of the airflow; this protects people on the other (upstream) side from the smoke.","Smoke stratification: initially, smoke forms a layer beneath the ceiling and clean air remains below; excessive airflow or applied water can disrupt this layer.","Backlayering: smoke beneath the ceiling flows back against the direction of ventilation when the air velocity is below the critical velocity.","Longitudinal ventilation: ventilation along the length of the tunnel using special jet fans that push the smoke in one direction.","Rate of development: fires involving cars and trucks in a tunnel can quickly generate very high temperatures."]},{heading:"3. Tunnel safety systems",body:"Protective systems:",points:["Alarm system: heat and smoke detectors, and alarm sounders.","Fire suppression: automatic water systems (deluge or water mist) and hose outlets for firefighters.","Ventilation: special fans for smoke extraction and smoke control.","Emergency exits: special doors and protected routes with illuminated signs.","Communications: an in-tunnel radio system and emergency telephones."]},{heading:"4. Rescue in tunnels",body:"How people are rescued:",points:["Pedestrian walkway: a dedicated route for escaping on foot.","Direction of escape: always toward fresh air and away from the smoke.","SCBA: self-contained breathing apparatus for all firefighters entering the tunnel.","Casualty sorting point (triage): outside the tunnel and in a safe location.","Traffic: closing the road to vehicles in coordination with the traffic police."]},{heading:"5. Training and planning",body:"Advance preparation:",points:["Emergency plan: a specific plan for each tunnel.","Training: periodic evacuation drills.","Maps: tunnel maps for firefighting teams.","Coordination: with the police and the ambulance service.","Experience: learning lessons from past incidents."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_30_tunnel_fire.mp4",title:"Tunnel fire",description:"Fire dynamics in tunnels"},{kind:"image",src:"media/images/uni_30_ventilation.png",title:"Ventilation",description:"The tunnel ventilation system"},{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_30_evacuation.mp4",title:"Evacuation",description:"Evacuating a tunnel"}],quiz:[{question:"Why is a fire in a tunnel more dangerous than one in an ordinary building?",options:[{text:"Ventilation is limited, escape is difficult and smoke accumulates",correct:!0},{text:"There is no difference",correct:!1},{text:"Ordinary buildings are worse",correct:!1},{text:"It is a normal situation",correct:!1}],explanation:"In a tunnel, ventilation is limited, exits are few and far apart, and smoke accumulates quickly beneath the low ceiling; all of this increases the danger."},{question:"What is backlayering?",options:[{text:"Ventilation",correct:!1},{text:"Smoke flowing back against the direction of ventilation",correct:!0},{text:"Rescue",correct:!1},{text:"Fire suppression",correct:!1}],explanation:"Backlayering is a phenomenon in which smoke beneath the ceiling flows back against the direction of ventilation when the air velocity is insufficient; this is very dangerous for occupants and teams."}]},{id:32,code:"UNI-332",icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers — complete training",subtitle:"Fire Extinguisher — Complete Training (PASS)",summary:"Identifying all types of extinguishers, fire classes under NFPA and EN 2, the PASS technique in detail and the inspection and maintenance of extinguishers.",blocks:[{body:"The fire extinguisher is an essential device for putting out small fires in their incipient stage. In universities and research laboratories, chemicals and electrical equipment raise the risk of fire. Every student and faculty member must be able to use an extinguisher quickly and correctly. Small extinguishers empty quickly, often in a few seconds, and the exact discharge time is on the label, so there is no time to lose and the technique must be right the first time. If the fire grows or smoke blocks the escape route, leave the extinguisher, get out and call 115."},{heading:"1. Fire classes according to NFPA",body:"Under the American NFPA system (NFPA 10), fires are divided into five classes. Classes here use NFPA 10 letters as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. EN 2 equivalents: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. The five NFPA classes:",points:["Class A (ordinary solid combustibles): wood, paper, fabric, plastic. Extinguishers: water, ABC powder, foam.","Class B (flammable liquids and gases): gasoline, oil, grease, alcohol, laboratory solvents, LPG. Liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder (never use water!); for alcohol, acetone and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop.","Class C (energized electrical equipment): switches, transformers, panels, generators, servers. Extinguishers: CO₂, ABC powder, clean agent. (Do not use water or foam unless the label shows a Class C rating or an electrical-test marking, as on some water-mist units!) If it is safe to do so, isolate the electrical supply first.","Class D (combustible metals): magnesium, powdered aluminum, titanium, lithium (metal). Extinguisher: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal (lithium, for example, needs an agent listed for lithium). Never use water, ordinary powder or foam (water can cause a violent reaction and an explosion!). Dry sand only if the site fire plan or the metal's safety data sheet names it and it is completely dry. Note: lithium-ion battery fires are not Class D; water and continuous cooling are recommended.","Class K (cooking oils; Class F under EN 2): cooking oils and fats. Extinguisher: wet chemical (in the UK it carries a yellow band under BS 7863; read the label). (Water causes burning oil to erupt violently and spreads the fire!)"]},{heading:"2. Types of extinguishers and their colors",body:"Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols. The main types:",points:["Water extinguisher: all red, no band. For Class A only (wood, paper). Never use it on electrical equipment or oil!","CO₂ extinguisher: black band. For Class B and live electrical equipment (NFPA B and C; under EN 2, C means gas and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires). A very cold gas is discharged and the discharge horn becomes extremely cold: hold only the handle, never the horn, which can cause a cold burn (frostbite) to your hand. CO₂ has a short range (see the label) and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.","ABC dry powder extinguisher: blue band. For Classes A, B and C (not for D and not for K/F). The most widespread type. Drawbacks: it reduces visibility and contaminates equipment.","Foam extinguisher (AFFF): cream band. For Classes A and B. It spreads foam over the liquid and cuts off the oxygen. Do not use it on electrical equipment! Note: AFFF contains PFAS and is being replaced by fluorine-free foam in many countries.","Clean agent extinguisher (portable): for sensitive equipment, laboratories and server rooms. The class rating depends on the model (often B and C, some also A), so read the label; the minimum room size is also on the label. It leaves no residue; leave the room after use.","Special extinguisher (Class D): for combustible metals — it contains a special agent that differs from metal to metal. Use only one whose label lists that metal, and use it as the label and the manufacturer's instructions say."]},{heading:"3. The PASS technique in detail",body:`Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions. Start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller. Never turn your back on the fire:`,points:["P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin prevents the extinguisher from being discharged by mistake.","A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the flames above!","S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle firmly so that the agent is discharged.","S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side across the base of the fire until the fire is completely out, and watch to make sure it does not reignite."]},{heading:"4. Inspection and maintenance of extinguishers (NFPA 10)",body:"Inspection and maintenance are five separate tasks; do not mix them up. The intervals below follow NFPA 10 and OSHA (29 CFR 1910.157); the rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed:",points:["Visual check (monthly, by the person in charge): in its place, visible and easy to reach; safety pin and tamper seal intact; label readable and facing out; no damage, rust or leaks; nozzle and hose not damaged or blocked.","Pressure gauge: the needle must be in the green zone, if there is a gauge. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full.","Inspection and maintenance (yearly, by a qualified person).","Recharge: after any use, even a short one, by a qualified person.","6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years.","Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder), by type: every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂, wet chemical and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. A hydrostatic test is not a replacement.","Removal from service: an extinguisher is taken out of service if it fails an inspection or test, is damaged, obsolete or recalled, has no readable label, or is a non-rechargeable unit past the service life on its label."]},{heading:"5. Specific hazards in universities",body:"Universities are sensitive environments because of:",points:["Chemistry laboratories: solvents and alcohols (Class B), and certain reactive metals (Class D).","Server and IT rooms: energized electrical equipment (Class C under NFPA): clean agent or CO₂.","University kitchens: cooking oil (Class K under NFPA / F under EN 2).","Libraries and archives: paper and wood (Class A).","Placement: place extinguishers near doors and corridors; under NFPA 10, the travel distance to reach an extinguisher in Class A areas must not exceed 23 meters (75 feet), and in Class B areas it is shorter (9–15 meters)."]}],media:[{kind:"video",src:"media/videos/uni_fire_ext.mp4",title:"The fire extinguisher",description:"The PASS technique and the types of extinguishers"}],quiz:[{question:"Under NFPA letters, for which fire classes is a CO₂ extinguisher suitable?",options:[{text:"A and D",correct:!1},{text:"B and C",correct:!0},{text:"A and K",correct:!1},{text:"D and K",correct:!1}],explanation:"Under NFPA letters, CO₂ is suitable for flammable liquids (B) and live electrical equipment (C) because it does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue. Under EN 2, C means gas, and CO₂ is not rated for gas fires."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where should the extinguisher nozzle be aimed?",options:[{text:"At the top of the flames",correct:!1},{text:"At the base of the fire",correct:!0},{text:"Away from the fire",correct:!1},{text:"At the ceiling",correct:!1}],explanation:"Always aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, because that is where the fuel is and where the fire is extinguished."},{question:"A fire involving a flammable chemical liquid breaks out in a university laboratory; which extinguisher do you use?",options:[{text:"Water",correct:!1},{text:"CO₂ or ABC powder",correct:!0},{text:"Special Class D powder",correct:!1},{text:"Wet chemical",correct:!1}],explanation:"A flammable liquid fire is Class B: CO₂ or ABC powder is suitable. Use it only if the fire is small and just starting, the alarm has been raised and 115 called, you are trained and your exit is behind you; if the fire grows, leave the area and call 115."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
